        'ARITH_PROFIT_LOSS',
        'CHEAT_SHEET',
        'FOUNDATION',
        4,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005'::uuid,
        4,
        'Comprehensive Profit, Loss and Discount Formula Reference Sheet: The complete mathematical framework linking Cost Price ($$\text{CP}$$), Marked Price ($$\text{MP}$$), Selling Price ($$\text{SP}$$), Markup Percentage ($$m\%$$), Discount Percentage ($$d\%$$), and Profit Percentage ($$p\%$$).',
        'Confusing Markup (calculated strictly on $$\text{CP}$$) with Discount (calculated strictly on $$\text{MP}$$).',
        '["Markup & Discount Bridge: $$\\frac{\\text{MP}}{\\text{CP}} = \\frac{100 + p\\%}{100 - d\\%}$$", "Selling Price from CP: $$\\text{SP} = \\text{CP} \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{p}{100}\\right)$$", "Selling Price from MP: $$\\text{SP} = \\text{MP} \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{d}{100}\\right)$$", "Dishonest Merchant Profit: $$\\text{Profit}\\% = \\left(\\frac{\\text{True Weight} - \\text{False Weight}}{\\text{False Weight}}\\right) \\times 100\\%$$", "Equal SP Equal Gain/Loss: $$\\text{Net Loss}\\% = \\left(\\frac{x}{10}\\right)^2\\% = \\frac{x^2}{100}\\%$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A shopkeeper marks an article $$40\\%$$ above cost price and allows a discount of $$25\\%$$ to customers. Using the bridge formula, determine his overall profit percentage.", "steps": ["Step 1: Express Markup multiplier: $$\\frac{\\text{MP}}{\\text{CP}} = 1 + \\frac{40}{100} = 1.40 = \\frac{7}{5}$$.", "Step 2: Express Discount multiplier: $$\\frac{\\text{SP}}{\\text{MP}} = 1 - \\frac{25}{100} = 0.75 = \\frac{3}{4}$$.", "Step 3: Combine multipliers to find $$\\frac{\\text{SP}}{\\text{CP}}$$: $$\\frac{\\text{SP}}{\\text{CP}} = \\frac{7}{5} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{21}{20}$$.", "Step 4: Compute Profit percentage: $$\\text{Profit}\\% = \\left(\\frac{21 - 20}{20}\\right) \\times 100\\% = \\frac{1}{20} \\times 100\\% = 5\\%$$. Alternatively, using the bridge formula: $$\\frac{140}{100} = \\frac{100 + p}{75} \\implies 100 + p = 1.40 \\times 75 = 105 \\implies p = 5\\%$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Profit} = 5\\%$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Identify Bridge Relation", "explanation": "Apply the ratio of MP to CP.", "formula": "$$\\frac{\\text{MP}}{\\text{CP}} = \\frac{100 + p}{100 - d}$$", "tip": "Directly links markup, discount, and profit."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Substitute Values", "explanation": "Substitute $$m = 40\\%$$ and $$d = 25\\%$$.", "formula": "$$\\frac{140}{100} = \\frac{100 + p}{75}$$", "tip": "Cross-multiply: $$1.40 \\times 75 = 105$$."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Extract Profit", "explanation": "Subtract 100 from 105.", "formula": "$$p = 105 - 100 = 5\\%$$", "tip": "Positive value confirms profit."}], "keyTakeaways": ["The bridge formula $$\\frac{\\text{MP}}{\\text{CP}} = \\frac{100 + p}{100 - d}$$ bypasses calculating intermediate cash values.", "Selling two items at the same price with $$+x\\%$$ and $$-x\\%$$ always results in a net loss of $$\\frac{x^2}{100}\\%$$, never zero."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Two cars are sold for Rs. 3,00,000 each. One makes a 20% gain, the other a 20% loss. What is the net percentage outcome?", "options": ["No profit no loss", "$$4\\%$$ loss", "$$4\\%$$ gain", "$$2\\%$$ loss"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "$$\\text{Net Loss}\\% = \\frac{20^2}{100}\\% = 4\\%\\text{ loss}$$. When selling prices are equal and percentage gain equals percentage loss, there is always an overall loss."}]}'
