        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000039'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Multiplier Scaling Trap in Simple Interest: When a problem states ''a sum of money doubles itself in $$T$$ years'', students mistakenly apply geometric powers instead of arithmetic multiples. If money becomes $$n$$ times in $$T$$ years, the interest earned is $$(n - 1)P$$, NOT $$nP$$. Therefore, to become $$m$$ times, the time required is $$T_m = T \times \frac{m - 1}{n - 1}$$. For instance, if money doubles ($$n=2$$) in 5 years, it earns $$1P$$ interest in 5 years; to quadruple ($$m=4$$), it must earn $$3P$$ interest, taking $$3 \times 5 = 15\text{ years}$$, NOT $$10\text{ years}$$!',
        'Assuming doubling in 5 years means quadrupling in 10 years by linear multiplication of total amount rather than interest accrued.',
        '["Interest Portion: $$\\text{Interest} = (n - 1) \\times P$$", "Scaling Time Formula: $$T_2 = T_1 \\times \\left(\\frac{m - 1}{n - 1}\\right)$$", "Unit Time Rate: $$R = \\frac{(n - 1) \\times 100}{T}\\%$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A sum of money invested under simple interest triples itself ($$3\\times$$) in $$8\\text{ years}$$. In how many years will the same sum become $$7\\text{ times}$$ itself at the same rate?", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify interest earned in first phase: A sum becoming 3 times means $$A_1 = 3P$$. Therefore, Simple Interest earned is $$I_1 = 3P - P = 2P$$.", "Step 2: Relate interest to time: The sum generates $$2P$$ interest in $$8\\text{ years}$$. Rate of interest accumulation is $$\\frac{2P}{8} = 0.25P\\text{ per year}$$.", "Step 3: Determine target interest requirement: To become 7 times ($$A_2 = 7P$$), the required interest is $$I_2 = 7P - P = 6P$$.", "Step 4: Calculate required time: Time required is $$\\frac{6P}{0.25P} = 24\\text{ years}$$. Alternatively, using formula: $$T_2 = 8 \\times \\left(\\frac{7 - 1}{3 - 1}\\right) = 8 \\times \\frac{6}{2} = 8 \\times 3 = 24\\text{ years}$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$T = 24\\text{ years}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Calculate Accrued Interest Multiple", "explanation": "Subtract principal from total amount to isolate pure interest.", "formula": "$$I_1 = 3P - P = 2P$$", "tip": "Tripling yields 2 times principal as interest."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Identify Target Interest Multiple", "explanation": "Target amount of 7 times requires 6 times principal.", "formula": "$$I_2 = 7P - P = 6P$$", "tip": "Seven times yields 6 times interest."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Apply Ratio of Interests", "explanation": "Multiply original duration by ratio of interest demands.", "formula": "$$T_2 = 8 \\times \\frac{6P}{2P} = 24\\text{ years}$$", "tip": "Avoid multiplying 8 by (7/3)."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Always subtract 1 from the multiplier because principal is never interest.", "Doubling takes $$1P$$ interest, tripling takes $$2P$$ interest, quadrupling takes $$3P$$ interest."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "A sum doubles in 6 years at simple interest. In how many years will it become 4 times itself?", "options": ["12 years", "18 years", "24 years", "15 years"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "$$T_2 = 6 \\times \\frac{4 - 1}{2 - 1} = 6 \\times 3 = 18\\text{ years}$$. In 6 years it gains 1P; to gain 3P takes $$6 \\times 3 = 18$$ years."}]}'
