        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Alternating Days and Work-Leaving Traps: In alternating work problems (A works on day 1, B on day 2), work progresses in discrete cycles. The critical trap is overshooting the final target work in the last fractional cycle. You must calculate the completed whole cycles first, determine the exact remaining work, and assign it only to the worker whose turn it is. In negative work problems (pipes and cisterns with a leak), the leak must be subtracted from inlet rates ($$\text{Net Rate} = R_{\text{fill}} - R_{\text{leak}}$$).',
        'Dividing total work directly by average rate in alternating work problems, ignoring that the faster worker might finish the job before the second worker even begins their turn.',
        '["Cycle Efficiency: $$W_{\\text{cycle}} = R_A + R_B \\quad (\\text{time} = 2\\text{ days})$$", "Remaining Work: $$W_{\\text{rem}} = W_{\\text{total}} - k \\times W_{\\text{cycle}}$$", "Last Fractional Day: $$t_{\\text{last}} = \\frac{W_{\\text{rem}}}{R_{\\text{active}}}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A can complete a project in $$12\\text{ days}$$ and B can complete it in $$18\\text{ days}$$. If they work on alternate days starting with A on Day 1, in how many total days will the work be completed?", "steps": ["Step 1: Set total work using LCM: $$\\text{LCM}(12, 18) = 36\\text{ units}$$. Efficiency of A is $$R_A = \\frac{36}{12} = 3\\text{ units/day}$$; efficiency of B is $$R_B = \\frac{36}{18} = 2\\text{ units/day}$$.", "Step 2: Determine 2-day cycle output: In 1 cycle of 2 days (A on Day 1, B on Day 2), work done is $$3 + 2 = 5\\text{ units}$$.", "Step 3: Calculate full cycles: In 7 full cycles ($$7 \\times 2 = 14\\text{ days}$$), total work completed is $$7 \\times 5 = 35\\text{ units}$$.", "Step 4: Resolve remaining work: Remaining work is $$36 - 35 = 1\\text{ unit}$$. On Day 15, it is A''s turn. A has an efficiency of 3 units/day, so A requires $$\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ day}$$. Total time is $$14 + \\frac{1}{3} = 14\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ days}$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Total Time} = 14\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ days}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Compute Unit Efficiencies", "explanation": "LCM(12, 18) = 36 units.", "formula": "$$R_A = 3, \\quad R_B = 2\\text{ units/day}$$", "tip": "Always work in integer units."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Calculate 2-Day Cycle Work", "explanation": "A and B together in one alternating cycle complete 5 units.", "formula": "$$W_{\\text{cycle}} = 5\\text{ units in 2 days}$$", "tip": "Do not divide 36 by 2.5 days."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Evaluate Complete Cycles", "explanation": "Find largest multiple of 5 <= 36: 7 cycles = 35 units in 14 days.", "formula": "$$7 \\times 5 = 35\\text{ units in } 14\\text{ days}$$", "tip": "Leaves exactly 1 unit."}, {"stepNumber": 4, "title": "Calculate Final Fractional Day", "explanation": "A works on Day 15 and finishes 1 unit in 1/3 day.", "formula": "$$14 + \\frac{1}{3} = 14\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ days}$$", "tip": "Do not give B a turn on Day 15."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Never divide total work by average daily rate in alternate work; evaluate discrete full cycles first.", "The person starting on Day 1 works on all odd days (1, 3, 5, ...)."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "A completes a work in 10 days, B in 20 days. They work alternately starting with A. How many days to complete 60 units (LCM)?", "options": ["13 days", "13.5 days", "14 days", "12 days"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "Efficiencies: A=6, B=3. Cycle of 2 days = 9 units. 6 cycles (12 days) = 54 units. Rem = 6 units. On day 13, A does 6 units. Total = 13 days."}]}'
