        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012'::uuid,
        6,
        'Trains Crossing and Relative Speed Calculations: When a train of length $$L_1$$ travelling at speed $$S_1$$ passes a second train of length $$L_2$$ travelling at speed $$S_2$$, the total distance traversed during the pass is strictly the sum of lengths ($$D = L_1 + L_2$$). If travelling in opposite directions, relative speed is $$S_{\text{rel}} = S_1 + S_2$$; if in the same direction, $$S_{\text{rel}} = |S_1 - S_2|$$. Always convert speeds from $$\text{km/h}$$ to $$\text{m/s}$$ by multiplying by $$\frac{5}{18}$$.',
        'Subtracting train lengths when travelling in the same direction. Train lengths ALWAYS add ($$L_1 + L_2$$), regardless of direction!',
        '["Speed Conversion: $$S_{\\text{m/s}} = S_{\\text{km/h}} \\times \\frac{5}{18}$$", "Opposite Direction: $$S_{\\text{rel}} = S_1 + S_2$$", "Same Direction: $$S_{\\text{rel}} = |S_1 - S_2|$$", "Time to Cross: $$T = \\frac{L_1 + L_2}{S_{\\text{rel}}}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Two trains of lengths $$150\\text{ m}$$ and $$250\\text{ m}$$ are running on parallel tracks. When moving in opposite directions, they pass each other in $$10\\text{ seconds}$$. If the first train runs at $$54\\text{ km/h}$$, find the speed of the second train in $$\\text{km/h}$$.", "steps": ["Step 1: Calculate total crossing distance: $$D = L_1 + L_2 = 150 + 250 = 400\\text{ meters}$$.", "Step 2: Determine relative speed in m/s: Since time taken is $$10\\text{ seconds}$$, $$S_{\\text{rel}} = \\frac{D}{T} = \\frac{400}{10} = 40\\text{ m/s}$$.", "Step 3: Convert relative speed to km/h: $$S_{\\text{rel}} = 40 \\times \\frac{18}{5} = 8 \\times 18 = 144\\text{ km/h}$$.", "Step 4: Solve for speed of second train ($$S_2$$): In opposite directions, $$S_{\\text{rel}} = S_1 + S_2$$. Given $$S_1 = 54\\text{ km/h}$$, we have $$54 + S_2 = 144 \\implies S_2 = 144 - 54 = 90\\text{ km/h}$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$S_2 = 90\\text{ km/h}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Sum Train Lengths", "explanation": "Total distance is sum of lengths.", "formula": "$$D = 150 + 250 = 400\\text{ m}$$", "tip": "Lengths always add."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Compute Relative Speed in m/s", "explanation": "Divide 400 m by 10 s.", "formula": "$$S_{\\text{rel}} = 40\\text{ m/s}$$", "tip": "40 m/s is the combined velocity."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Convert to km/h", "explanation": "Multiply by 18/5.", "formula": "$$40 \\times \\frac{18}{5} = 144\\text{ km/h}$$", "tip": "8 * 18 = 144."}, {"stepNumber": 4, "title": "Extract Second Train Speed", "explanation": "Subtract S1 from S_rel.", "formula": "$$S_2 = 144 - 54 = 90\\text{ km/h}$$", "tip": "Check: 54 + 90 = 144 km/h."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Total distance covered when two objects cross is ALWAYS $$L_1 + L_2$$, never $$L_1 - L_2$$.", "Multiply by $$\\frac{5}{18}$$ to convert $$\\text{km/h} \\to \\text{m/s}$$; multiply by $$\\frac{18}{5}$$ to convert $$\\text{m/s} \\to \\text{km/h}$$."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is 72 km/h expressed in m/s?", "options": ["15 m/s", "20 m/s", "25 m/s", "30 m/s"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "$$72 \\times \\frac{5}{18} = 4 \\times 5 = 20\\text{ m/s}$$."}]}'
