        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013'::uuid,
        4,
        'Quadratic Equations and Sign Rules Cheat Sheet: Rapid-fire root inspection rules enabling exam candidates to determine inequality relationships without calculating roots. Sign shortcut rule: When both constant terms ($$c_1, c_2$$) are negative, the relation is ALWAYS ''Cannot be determined (CND)''.',
        'Spending time factoring equations where constant terms are negative, when the answer is deterministically CND.',
        '["Equation Sign Form: $$ax^2 + bx + c = 0$$", "Sign Rule (+, +): $$(+, +) \\implies \\text{Both roots are negative } (-, -)$$", "Sign Rule (-, +): $$(-, +) \\implies \\text{Both roots are positive } (+, +)$$", "Sign Rule (\\pm, -): $$(\\pm, -) \\implies \\text{Roots have opposite signs } (+, -)$$", "CND Rule: If both equations end in negative constants ($$c_1 < 0, c_2 < 0$$), answer is CND!"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Compare $$x$$ and $$y$$ without calculating roots:\nI. $$x^2 + 2x - 35 = 0$$\nII. $$y^2 - 4y - 21 = 0$$", "steps": ["Step 1: Inspect the constant term of Equation I: $$c = -35$$ (negative). This means the roots of $$x$$ have opposite signs: one positive and one negative ($$x_1 > 0, x_2 < 0$$).", "Step 2: Inspect the constant term of Equation II: $$c = -21$$ (negative). This means the roots of $$y$$ have opposite signs: one positive and one negative ($$y_1 > 0, y_2 < 0$$).", "Step 3: Apply the Negative Constant Rule: When comparing two equations each having both a positive and a negative root, the positive root of $$x$$ is greater than the negative root of $$y$$, but the negative root of $$x$$ is less than the positive root of $$y$$.", "Step 4: Conclude relation: Overlapping signs create contradictory inequalities ($$x > y$$ and $$x < y$$). Therefore, no definite relationship can be established (CND)."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Relationship cannot be determined (CND)}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Inspect Signs of Equation I", "explanation": "Constant term -35 is negative.", "formula": "$$\\text{Roots of } x \\implies (+, -)$$", "tip": "Opposite sign roots."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Inspect Signs of Equation II", "explanation": "Constant term -21 is negative.", "formula": "$$\\text{Roots of } y \\implies (+, -)$$", "tip": "Opposite sign roots."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Apply CND Shortcut", "explanation": "Both constants negative guarantees overlapping intervals.", "formula": "$$\\text{Relation} = \\text{CND}$$", "tip": "Solved in 3 seconds without factoring."}], "keyTakeaways": ["If the constant term $$c$$ is negative in both equations, the answer is ALWAYS ''Cannot be determined''.", "If $$x$$ equation has $$(-, +)$$ (roots $$+, +$$) and $$y$$ equation has $$(+, +)$$ (roots $$-, -$$), then $$x > y$$ instantly."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the relation between x and y if x^2 - 7x + 12 = 0 and y^2 + 9y + 20 = 0?", "options": ["x > y", "x < y", "x >= y", "CND"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Roots of x are (+, +) = (+3, +4). Roots of y are (-, -) = (-4, -5). Every positive root is strictly greater than every negative root, so $$x > y$$."}]}'
