        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032'::uuid,
        5,
        'The ''Only A Few'' and ''Can Never Be'' Traps in Syllogisms: In banking exams, ''Only a few A are B'' is a dual-statement quantifier: it strictly means BOTH ''Some A are B'' AND ''Some A are NOT B''. Therefore, the conclusion ''All A can be B is a possibility'' is DEFINITIVELY FALSE! However, ''All B can be A is a possibility'' CAN BE TRUE unless restricted otherwise. Furthermore, ''Can never be'' represents a definite negative conclusion ($$\text{Some } \dots \text{ are not}$$), NOT a possibility test.',
        'Treating ''Only a few A are B'' as synonymous with standard ''Some A are B'', allowing ''All A being B'' as a possibility.',
        '["Only A few A are B: $$\\text{Some } A \\text{ are } B \\quad \\land \\quad \\text{Some } A \\text{ are NOT } B$$", "All A can be B: $$\\text{Definitely FALSE}$$", "All B can be A: $$\\text{Possibility can be TRUE}$$", "Can never be: $$\\text{Definite statement, NOT a possibility}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Statements:\n1. Only a few Books are Pens.\n2. All Pens are Pencils.\n\nConclusions:\nI. All Books can be Pens.\nII. Some Pencils are Books.", "steps": ["Step 1: Deconstruct ''Only a few Books are Pens'': This explicitly asserts that some Books are Pens, AND there is a part of Books that can NEVER be Pens.", "Step 2: Evaluate Conclusion I (''All Books can be Pens''): Since a portion of Books is forbidden from entering Pens by the quantifier ''Only a few'', it is impossible for all Books to become Pens. Conclusion I is FALSE.", "Step 3: Evaluate Conclusion II (''Some Pencils are Books''): All Pens are inside Pencils, and Some Books are Pens. The overlapping region of Books and Pens is completely inside Pencils. Therefore, Some Pencils are definitely Books. Conclusion II is TRUE."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Only Conclusion II follows}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Analyze ''Only a few''", "explanation": "Breaks into Some and Some Not.", "formula": "$$\\text{Only a few } A \\text{ are } B \\implies A \\not\\subseteq B$$", "tip": "Prevents All A from being B."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Evaluate Conclusion I", "explanation": "All Books in Pens contradicts ''Only a few''.", "formula": "$$\\text{Conclusion I} = \\text{False}$$", "tip": "Definite violation."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Evaluate Conclusion II", "explanation": "Overlap between Books and Pens is inside Pencils.", "formula": "$$\\text{Conclusion II} = \\text{True}$$", "tip": "Directly supported by diagram."}], "keyTakeaways": ["''Only a few A are B'' forbids all A from ever being inside B.", "All B can still be inside A without violating the condition."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Statement: Only a few Cats are Dogs. Can all Dogs be Cats?", "options": ["Yes, it is possible", "No, it is impossible", "Definitely true", "Cannot be determined"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Yes! Only a few Cats are Dogs restricts Cats from fully entering Dogs, but Dogs can freely be completely inside Cats."}]}'
