        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Decimal Multiplier and Wrong-Number Series Traps: In modern exam series, two subtle patterns frequently mislead candidates: (1) Decimal multipliers ($$\times 0.5, \times 1, \times 1.5, \times 2$$ or $$\times 0.5 + 0.5, \times 1 + 1$$), recognizable whenever a number is immediately followed by a number nearly half its size or approximately equal to itself; (2) In ''Wrong Number'' series, a single flawed term corrupts TWO consecutive differences, leading candidates to falsely blame the neighboring correct term.',
        'Assuming an arithmetic difference when a series starts with a reduction (e.g., 20, 10, 10, 15, 30), which is almost universally a $$\times 0.5, \times 1, \times 1.5$$ pattern.',
        '["Decimal Reduction Marker: $$T_2 \\approx 0.5 \\times T_1 \\implies \\text{Decimal multiplier}$$", "Standard Multiplier Progression: $$\\times 0.5, \\times 1, \\times 1.5, \\times 2, \\times 2.5$$", "Wrong Term Isolation: $$\\text{Flawed term alters two consecutive differences } \\Delta_i, \\Delta_{i+1}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Identify the pattern and find the next term: $$12, \\; 6, \\; 6, \\; 9, \\; 18, \\; 45, \\; ?$$", "steps": ["Step 1: Observe initial progression: $$12 \\to 6$$ is a halving. $$6 \\to 6$$ is multiplication by 1.", "Step 2: Check ratios of successive terms:\n- $$6 / 12 = 0.5$$\n- $$6 / 6 = 1.0$$\n- $$9 / 6 = 1.5$$\n- $$18 / 9 = 2.0$$\n- $$45 / 18 = 2.5$$", "Step 3: Extrapolate pattern: The multipliers form an arithmetic progression: $$0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5$$. The next multiplier is $$3.0$$.", "Step 4: Compute next term: $$45 \\times 3.0 = 135$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Next Term} = 135$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Detect Multiplier Behavior", "explanation": "First term halves, second term repeats.", "formula": "$$12 \\times 0.5 = 6, \\quad 6 \\times 1 = 6$$", "tip": "Classic half-step signature."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Confirm Progression", "explanation": "Check later multipliers.", "formula": "$$\\times 1.5, \\times 2.0, \\times 2.5$$", "tip": "Increments by 0.5 each step."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Calculate Missing Term", "explanation": "Multiply 45 by 3.0.", "formula": "$$45 \\times 3 = 135$$", "tip": "45 * 3 = 135."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Any series exhibiting $$N, \\; N/2, \\; N/2$$ is driven by $$\\times 0.5, \\times 1.0, \\dots$$.", "In wrong number series, the incorrect term is between the two irregular difference steps."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the next term in: 8, 4, 4, 6, 12, ?", "options": ["24", "30", "18", "36"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "Multipliers are: *0.5, *1, *1.5, *2. Next is *2.5 => $$12 \\times 2.5 = 30$$."}]}'
    ),
    (
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000057'::uuid,
        'REAS',
        'SERIES',
        'SER_NUMBER',
        'CHEAT_SHEET',
        'FOUNDATION',
        4,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033'::uuid,
        4,
        'Number Series Pattern Taxonomy Reference Sheet: A structured classification of the seven core sequence generators encountered in competitive examinations (Difference, Double Difference, Squares & Cubes ($$n^2 \pm k, n^3 \pm n$$), Alternate Interleaved, Multiplier + Constant ($$\times a + b$$), Triangular Numbers, and Fibonacci additions).',
        'Forgetting to check the differences of differences when a single difference layer does not reveal a pattern.',
        '["Cubes with Offset: $$n^3 \\pm n \\implies 0, 6, 24, 60, 120, 210, 336, 504, 720$$", "Squares with Offset: $$n^2 \\pm 1 \\implies 0, 3, 8, 15, 24, 35, 48, 63, 80$$", "Double Difference: $$\\Delta_2 = \\text{constant} \\implies \\text{Quadratic progression}$$", "Interleaved Series: $$\\text{Odd positions and even positions follow independent rules}$$", "Fibonacci Sum: $$T_n = T_{n-1} + T_{n-2}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Identify the underlying pattern in the sequence: $$0, \\; 6, \\; 24, \\; 60, \\; 120, \\; 210, \\; ?$$", "steps": ["Step 1: Test prime powers / cube relationships: Compare terms to standard cubes ($$1^3=1, 2^3=8, 3^3=27, 4^3=64, 5^3=125, 6^3=216$$).", "Step 2: Observe offset: Each term is exactly the cube of its index minus the index itself: $$n^3 - n$$.\n- $$1^3 - 1 = 0$$\n- $$2^3 - 2 = 6$$\n- $$3^3 - 3 = 24$$\n- $$4^3 - 4 = 60$$\n- $$5^3 - 5 = 120$$\n- $$6^3 - 6 = 210$$", "Step 3: Compute next term for $$n=7$$: $$7^3 - 7 = 343 - 7 = 336$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Next Term} = 336$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Recognize Canonical Numbers", "explanation": "Terms are near standard cubic numbers.", "formula": "$$0, 6, 24, 60, 120, 210$$", "tip": "Memorize the n^3 - n sequence."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Formulate Explicit Rule", "explanation": "Rule is n^3 - n.", "formula": "$$T_n = n^3 - n$$", "tip": "Fast recognition in 2 seconds."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Evaluate 7th Term", "explanation": "7^3 = 343; subtract 7.", "formula": "$$343 - 7 = 336$$", "tip": "336 is 7 * 6 * 8."}], "keyTakeaways": ["The sequence $$0, 6, 24, 60, 120, 210, 336$$ represents $$n(n-1)(n+1) = n^3 - n$$.", "Always check $$n^3 + n$$ ($$2, 10, 30, 68, 130, 222$$) as well."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the 5th term in the sequence generated by n^3 + n?", "options": ["125", "130", "120", "135"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "$$5^3 + 5 = 125 + 5 = 130$$."}]}'
    ),
    (
