        5,
        'Multi-Statement Chain Assembly: When statements are presented in fragmented pieces ($$A > B, C \le D, B = C$$), they must be linked into an unbroken unified chain before evaluating conclusions ($$A > B = C \le D$$). To test whether $$X > Y$$ holds, every intermediate link from $$X$$ to $$Y$$ must face the same direction, and AT LEAST ONE strictly dominant sign ($$>$$) must be present along the path. If only $$\ge$$ exists, then $$X > Y$$ is doubtful and cannot be asserted definitely.',
        'Declaring $$A > C$$ as definitely true when the path contains only $$A \ge B \ge C$$, which guarantees only $$A \ge C$$, not strict inequality.',
        '["Chain Link Rule: $$\\text{Unify variables through common nodes}$$", "Strict Dominance: $$>$$ or $$<$$ requires at least one strictly dominant sign along path", "Weak Dominance: $$\\ge$$ requires ALL signs along path to be $$\\ge$$ or $$=$$", "Opposing Clashing Signs: $$> \\dots < \\implies \\text{Relationship blocked}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Statements: $$P > Q \\ge R$$, $$R = S$$, $$S < T$$.\nConclusions:\nI. $$P > S$$\nII. $$R < T$$", "steps": ["Step 1: Assemble unified statement chain: Link through common variables $$R$$ and $$S$$: $$P > Q \\ge R = S < T$$.", "Step 2: Evaluate Conclusion I ($$P > S$$): Inspect path from $$P$$ to $$S$$: $$P > Q \\ge R = S$$. All symbols face right ($$>$$ and $$\\ge$$). A Priority 1 dominant sign ($$>$$) is present between $$P$$ and $$Q$$. Therefore, $$P > S$$ is DEFINITELY TRUE.", "Step 3: Evaluate Conclusion II ($$R < T$$): Inspect path from $$R$$ to $$T$$: $$R = S < T$$. All symbols face left ($$=$$ and $$<$$). A Priority 1 dominant sign ($$<$$) is present between $$S$$ and $$T$$. Therefore, $$R < T$$ is DEFINITELY TRUE.", "Step 4: Conclude: Both Conclusions I and II follow."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Both Conclusions I and II follow}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Merge Statements", "explanation": "Link statements through shared variables R and S.", "formula": "$$P > Q \\ge R = S < T$$", "tip": "Form a continuous chain."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Evaluate P to S Path", "explanation": "Path contains > and >=; dominant > prevails.", "formula": "$$P > S \\implies \\text{True}$$", "tip": "Strict inequality verified."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Evaluate R to T Path", "explanation": "Path contains = and <; dominant < prevails.", "formula": "$$R < T \\implies \\text{True}$$", "tip": "Strict inequality verified."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Always combine fragmented inequality statements into one continuous chain before evaluating conclusions.", "A strict inequality ($$>$$) holds if and only if all signs point the same way and at least one strictly dominant ($$>$$) sign exists."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "If A >= B and B >= C, is A > C definitely true?", "options": ["Yes", "No", "Cannot be determined", "Only if A = B"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "No! The path contains only >= symbols, which guarantees 