        4,
        'The 3-Sign ''Either-Or'' Trap in Blocked Chains: When two variables are separated by opposing signs ($$A > B < C$$), no definite relationship exists, meaning ALL THREE outcomes ($$A > C, A = C, A < C$$) are possible. If two conclusions collectively cover all three possibilities ($$A > C$$ and $$A \le C$$), they form a valid ''Either-Or'' pair! However, if they cover only two possibilities ($$A > C$$ and $$A < C$$, omitting $$A = C$$), ''Either-Or'' DOES NOT APPLY and ''Neither'' is the correct answer!',
        'Selecting ''Either-Or'' for $$A > C$$ and $$A < C$$ when the path is blocked, forgetting that $$A = C$$ is also possible.',
        '["Complete 3-Sign Cover: $$(A > C) \\lor (A \\le C) \\implies \\text{Either-Or follows (covers } >, <, =)$$", "Incomplete 2-Sign Cover (Trap!): $$(A > C) \\lor (A < C) \\implies \\text{Neither follows (omits } A=C)$$", "2-Sign Cover for Unbroken Chain: $$A \\ge B \\implies (A > B) \\lor (A = B) \\implies \\text{Either-Or follows}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Statement: $$X > Y < Z$$\n\nConclusions:\nI. $$X > Z$$\nII. $$X \\le Z$$", "steps": ["Step 1: Check path from $$X$$ to $$Z$$: Opposing signs ($$>$$ and $$<$$) exist between $$X$$ and $$Z$$. Path is blocked. Neither conclusion is definitely true individually.", "Step 2: Check coverage of variables: Conclusion I asserts $$X > Z$$. Conclusion II asserts $$X \\le Z$$ (which means $$X < Z$$ OR $$X = Z$$).", "Step 3: Evaluate combined coverage: Together, the two conclusions cover all three logical possibilities ($$>, <, =$$). Because $$X$$ must be greater than, less than, or equal to $$Z$$, exactly one of them MUST be true. Therefore, ''Either Conclusion I or II follows''."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Either Conclusion I or II follows}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Check Path Validity", "explanation": "Opposing signs make both conclusions indeterminate individually.", "formula": "$$X > Y < Z \\implies \\text{Indeterminate}$$", "tip": "Path is blocked."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Check Signs Coverage", "explanation": "Conclusion I is >, Conclusion II is <= (< and =).", "formula": "$$>, <, = \\text{ all covered}$$", "tip": "Covers all 3 possibilities."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Conclude Either-Or", "explanation": "Since all 3 cases covered, Either-Or is valid.", "formula": "$$\\text{Either I or II}$$", "tip": "If II was X < Z, answer would be NEITHER."}], "keyTakeaways": ["When path has opposing signs, ''Either-Or'' requires covering all 3 signs ($$>, <, =$$).", "When path has unbroken $$\\ge$$ (e.g. $$A \\ge B$$), ''Either-Or'' requires covering only 2 signs ($$>$$ and $$=$$)."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "If A > B < C, and conclusions are (1) A > C, (2) A < C, what is the answer?", "options": ["Neither follows", "Either follows", "Both follow", "Only 1 follows"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Because $$A = C$$ is omitted, the two conclusions do not cover all possibilities. Thus, NEITHER follows."}]}'
