- Obtuse: $$a^2 + b^2 < c^2$$
Candidates frequently forget to check if the side lengths form a valid triangle first before applying the square test.',
        'Declaring sides like 2, 3, 6 as obtuse because $$2^2 + 3^2 < 6^2$$, when in fact they cannot form a triangle at all ($$2 + 3 = 5 < 6$$)!',
        '["Existence Condition: $$|a - b| < c < a + b$$", "Acute Triangle: $$a^2 + b^2 > c^2 \\quad (c = \\text{longest side})$$", "Right Triangle: $$a^2 + b^2 = c^2$$", "Obtuse Triangle: $$a^2 + b^2 < c^2 \\quad (\\text{provided } a+b > c)$$", "Degenerate Collinear: $$a + b = c \\implies \\text{Straight line, area } = 0$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Can the side lengths $$4\\text{ cm}$$, $$7\\text{ cm}$$, and $$12\\text{ cm}$$ form an obtuse triangle?", "steps": ["Step 1: Check triangle existence condition first: For any valid triangle, the sum of the two shorter sides must exceed the longest side: $$a + b > c$$.", "Step 2: Evaluate the sum of shorter sides: $$4 + 7 = 11\\text{ cm}$$.", "Step 3: Compare with longest side: $$11 < 12$$. Since the sum of the two shorter sides is less than the third side, the segments cannot meet to enclose any area.", "Step 4: Conclude: These lengths CANNOT form any triangle at all. Note: even though $$4^2 + 7^2 = 16 + 49 = 65 < 144$$, one must NEVER classify it as an obtuse triangle because the figure does not exist."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{No, cannot form any triangle (violates triangle inequality)}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Test Triangle Inequality", "explanation": "Check if sum of two smaller sides exceeds largest.", "formula": "$$4 + 7 = 11 < 12$$", "tip": "Crucial prerequisite check."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Avoid Square Pitfall", "explanation": "Do not evaluate 4^2 + 7^2 < 12^2 without existence check.", "formula": "$$a + b \\le c \\implies \\text{Invalid}$$", "tip": "Saves candidates from false obtuse selection."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Always verify $$a + b > c$$ before applying any classification formula ($$a^2 + b^2 \\gtrless c^2$$).", "The number of possible integer triangles with two sides given as $$a$$ and $$b$$ is $$2 \\times \\min(a, b) - 1$$."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "How many distinct integer triangles can be formed with sides 5 cm and 9 cm as two of the sides?", "options": ["9", "10", "11", "7"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Number of integer triangles $$= 2 \\times \\min(5, 9) - 1 = 2(5) - 1 = 9$$. (Third side can range from $$9-5+1=5$$ to $$9+5-1=13$$, exactly 9 values)."}]}'
