        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Apparent Weight in Accelerating Elevators and Pseudo-Force Trap: An observer in an accelerating elevator experiences a pseudo-force ($$F_{\text{pseudo}} = -m a$$) acting opposite to the elevator''s acceleration. For upward acceleration ($$+a$$), the normal reaction (apparent weight) increases: $$N = m(g + a)$$. For downward acceleration ($$-a$$), apparent weight decreases: $$N = m(g - a)$$. In free-fall ($$a = g$$), $$N = 0$$ (weightlessness). Crucial exam trap: When an elevator moves downward with DECELERATION (slowing down), its acceleration is directed UPWARD ($$+a$$), meaning apparent weight increases ($$N = m(g + a)$$)!',
        'Equating downward motion with downward acceleration. An elevator moving down but slowing down has upward acceleration ($$a > 0$$), making the scale reading greater than true weight.',
        '["Upward Acceleration: $$N = m(g + a) > mg$$ (Feels heavier)", "Downward Acceleration: $$N = m(g - a) < mg$$ (Feels lighter)", "Constant Velocity ($$a = 0$$): $$N = mg$$ (Feels normal weight)", "Free Fall ($$a = g$$): $$N = 0$$ (True weightlessness)", "Downward Deceleration: $$a$$ is directed UPWARD $$\\implies N = m(g + a)$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A man of mass $$70\\text{ kg}$$ stands on a weighing scale in an elevator. What does the scale register (in Newtons) when the elevator is moving downwards and slowing down with a deceleration of $$2\\text{ m/s}^2$$? (Take $$g = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$$)", "steps": ["Step 1: Determine direction of acceleration: The elevator moves downward, but its speed is decreasing (decelerating). Deceleration in the direction of motion corresponds to acceleration directed opposite to velocity, meaning acceleration is directed UPWARD: $$a = +2\\text{ m/s}^2$$.", "Step 2: Apply the dynamic equilibrium equation for upward acceleration: $$N - mg = ma \\implies N = m(g + a)$$.", "Step 3: Substitute parameters: $$N = 70(10 + 2) = 70 \\times 12 = 840\\text{ N}$$. Note: If candidate naively assumed downward motion meant $$g - a$$, they would calculate $$70(10 - 2) = 560\\text{ N}$$, falling into the classic deceleration trap."], "finalAnswer": "$$N = 840\\text{ N}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Analyze Acceleration Vector", "explanation": "Downward velocity + deceleration = upward acceleration vector.", "formula": "$$\\vec{a} = +2\\text{ m/s}^2 \\text{ (upward)}$$", "tip": "Slowing down flips the effective acceleration vector."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Setup Apparent Weight Equation", "explanation": "Normal reaction balances gravity plus inertial force.", "formula": "$$N = m(g + a)$$", "tip": "Upward acceleration increases floor pressure."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Calculate Force Value", "explanation": "70 kg * 12 m/s^2 = 840 N.", "formula": "$$N = 70 \\times 12 = 840\\text{ N}$$", "tip": "Scale reads higher than stationary 700 N."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Apparent weight depends strictly on ACCELERATION, not on velocity.", "Moving downwards while slowing down creates an upward acceleration ($$N = m(g + a)$$)."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the apparent weight of a 60 kg person in an elevator moving upward with constant speed of 5 m/s? (g = 10 m/s^2)", "options": ["900 N", "600 N", "300 N", "Zero"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "Constant speed means acceleration $$a = 0$$. Therefore $$N = mg = 60 \\times 10 = 600\\text{ N}$$."}]}'
    ),
