        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017'::uuid,
        4,
        'Classical Mechanics & Kinematics Formula Cheat Sheet: Core formulas covering rectilinear motion under uniform acceleration, Newton''s laws, impulse-momentum theorem, work-energy-power, and projectile trajectories.',
        'Applying kinematic equations ($$v = u + at$$) when acceleration is variable rather than constant.',
        '["Kinematic 1: $$v = u + at$$", "Kinematic 2: $$s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$$", "Kinematic 3: $$v^2 - u^2 = 2as$$", "Distance in n-th second: $$s_n = u + \\frac{a}{2}(2n - 1)$$", "Impulse & Momentum: $$J = F \\Delta t = \\Delta p = m(v - u)$$", "Work-Energy Theorem: $$W_{\\text{net}} = \\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2}m v_f^2 - \\frac{1}{2}m v_i^2$$", "Projectile Maximum Height: $$H = \\frac{u^2 \\sin^2 \\theta}{2g}$$", "Projectile Range: $$R = \\frac{u^2 \\sin(2\\theta)}{g}$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A projectile is launched with an initial velocity of $$20\\text{ m/s}$$ at an angle of $$30^\\circ$$ to the horizontal. Calculate its maximum height and horizontal range. (Take $$g = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$$)", "steps": ["Step 1: Calculate Maximum Height ($$H$$): Formula is $$H = \\frac{u^2 \\sin^2 \\theta}{2g}$$. Since $$\\sin 30^\\circ = 0.5$$, $$\\sin^2 30^\\circ = 0.25$$. Therefore, $$H = \\frac{20^2 \\times 0.25}{2 \\times 10} = \\frac{400 \\times 0.25}{20} = \\frac{100}{20} = 5\\text{ meters}$$.", "Step 2: Calculate Horizontal Range ($$R$$): Formula is $$R = \\frac{u^2 \\sin(2\\theta)}{g}$$. For $$\\theta = 30^\\circ$$, $$2\\theta = 60^\\circ$$, and $$\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 0.866$$.", "Step 3: Evaluate range: $$R = \\frac{400 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{10} = 40 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 20\\sqrt{3} \\approx 34.64\\text{ meters}$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$H = 5\\text{ m}, \\quad R = 20\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m} \\approx 34.64\\text{ m}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Compute Vertical Apex Height", "explanation": "Substitute into H = u^2 sin^2(theta) / (2g).", "formula": "$$H = \\frac{400 \\times 0.25}{20} = 5\\text{ m}$$", "tip": "sin(30) = 1/2."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Compute Horizontal Range", "explanation": "Substitute into R = u^2 sin(2*theta) / g.", "formula": "$$R = \\frac{400 \\times \\sin(60^\\circ)}{10} = 20\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m}$$", "tip": "Maximum range occurs at 45 degrees."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Maximum range occurs at $$\\theta = 45^\\circ$$, where $$\\sin(2\\theta) = 1$$.", "Complementary launch angles ($$\\theta$$ and $$90^\\circ - \\theta$$) produce identical horizontal ranges."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "At what launch angle is the horizontal range of a projectile equal to four times its maximum height?", "options": ["45 degrees", "30 degrees", "60 degrees", "90 degrees"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "$$R = 4H \\cot \\theta$$. When $$R = 4H$$, $$\\cot \\theta = 1 \\implies \\theta = 45^\\circ$$."}]}'
    ),
