        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000069'::uuid,
        5,
        'Refraction, Total Internal Reflection (TIR) and Apparent Depth Traps: Light refracts according to Snell''s Law ($$n_1 \sin \theta_1 = n_2 \sin \theta_2$$). Two critical exam traps:
1. Total Internal Reflection occurs ONLY when light travels from a DENSER medium to a RARER medium ($$n_1 > n_2$$) and the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle ($$\theta > \theta_c$$, where $$\sin \theta_c = \frac{n_2}{n_1}$$). TIR CANNOT happen when light travels from air into glass/water!
2. When observing an underwater object from air, apparent depth is shallower than real depth: $$d_{\text{apparent}} = \frac{d_{\text{real}}}{n}$$. If observing from water into air, the object appears farther away ($$d_{\text{apparent}} = n \times d_{\text{real}}$$)!',
        'Asserting that total internal reflection can happen when light strikes water from air. Light must originate inside the denser medium.',
        '["TIR Conditions: (1) Denser to rarer medium ($$n_1 > n_2$$); (2) $$\\theta_i > \\theta_c$$", "Critical Angle Formula: $$\\sin \\theta_c = \\frac{n_{\\text{rare}}}{n_{\\text{dense}}}$$", "Apparent Depth (Looking into denser medium): $$d_{\\text{app}} = \\frac{d_{\\text{real}}}{n}$$", "Normal Shift: $$\\Delta d = d_{\\text{real}}\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\right)$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A swimming pool has a real depth of $$4.0\\text{ meters}$$. To an observer standing directly above in air, what is the apparent depth of the pool floor? (Refractive index of water $$n = \\frac{4}{3}$$)", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify optical configuration: Observer is in a rarer medium (air, $$n=1$$) looking into a denser medium (water, $$n = 4/3$$).", "Step 2: Apply the apparent depth formula: $$d_{\\text{apparent}} = \\frac{d_{\\text{real}}}{n}$$.", "Step 3: Substitute values: $$d_{\\text{apparent}} = \\frac{4.0}{\\frac{4}{3}} = 4.0 \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 3.0\\text{ meters}$$. The pool appears $$1.0\\text{ meter}$$ shallower than it actually is."], "finalAnswer": "$$d_{\\text{apparent}} = 3.0\\text{ meters}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Select Medium Configuration", "explanation": "Observer in air looks into water; apparent depth decreases.", "formula": "$$d_{\\text{app}} = \\frac{d_{\\text{real}}}{n}$$", "tip": "Light rays bend away from normal upon exiting water."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Compute Apparent Depth", "explanation": "Divide 4.0 by 4/3.", "formula": "$$4.0 \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 3.0\\text{ m}$$", "tip": "Shift is 4.0 - 3.0 = 1.0 m."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Underwater objects viewed from air appear closer by a factor of $$\\frac{1}{n}$$.", "Optical fibres, mirages, and diamond brilliance all rely fundamentally on Total Internal Reflection."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "If refractive index of glass is 1.5, what is the critical angle for glass-air interface?", "options": ["sin^(-1)(2/3) ~ 41.8 deg", "sin^(-1)(3/2)", "45 deg", "30 deg"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "$$\\sin \\theta_c = \\frac{1}{n} = \\frac{1}{1.5} = \\frac{2}{3} \\approx 0.667 \\implies \\theta_c \\approx 41.8^\\circ$$."}]}'
    ),
