        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019'::uuid,
        4,
        'Geometrical Optics & Wave Phenonema Reference Sheet: Essential formula compendium covering spherical mirrors, thin lenses, optical power combinations, dispersion, and vision defect corrections (Myopia vs Hypermetropia).',
        'Prescribing convex lenses for myopia. Myopia (near-sightedness) requires a diverging CONCAVE lens; Hypermetropia (far-sightedness) requires a converging CONVEX lens.',
        '["Mirror Formula: $$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u}$$", "Mirror Magnification: $$m = -\\frac{v}{u}$$", "Lens Formula: $$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u}$$", "Lens Magnification: $$m = +\\frac{v}{u}$$", "Power Combination: $$P_{\\text{net}} = P_1 + P_2 + \\dots$$", "Myopia Correction: Concave lens (negative power, $$P < 0$$)", "Hypermetropia Correction: Convex lens (positive power, $$P > 0$$)", "Cauchy Dispersion: $$n(\\lambda) = A + \\frac{B}{\\lambda^2}$$ (Violet bends most, Red bends least)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Two thin lenses of optical powers $$+3.5\\text{ D}$$ and $$-1.5\\text{ D}$$ are placed in co-axial contact. What is the net optical power and equivalent focal length of the combination?", "steps": ["Step 1: Calculate net optical power: $$P_{\\text{net}} = P_1 + P_2 = +3.5 + (-1.5) = +2.0\\text{ Diopters}$$.", "Step 2: Calculate equivalent focal length in meters: $$f = \\frac{1}{P_{\\text{net}}} = \\frac{1}{2.0} = 0.5\\text{ meters}$$.", "Step 3: Convert focal length to centimeters: $$f = 0.5 \\times 100 = +50\\text{ cm}$$. The combination acts as a converging (convex) lens."], "finalAnswer": "$$P_{\\text{net}} = +2.0\\text{ D}, \\quad f = +50\\text{ cm} \\quad (\\text{Converging})$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Sum Optical Powers", "explanation": "Powers in contact add algebraically.", "formula": "$$P_{\\text{net}} = 3.5 - 1.5 = +2.0\\text{ D}$$", "tip": "Retain algebraic signs."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Calculate Focal Length", "explanation": "f = 1 / P in meters.", "formula": "$$f = \\frac{100}{2.0} = 50\\text{ cm}$$", "tip": "Positive focal length confirms converging system."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Lenses in contact have their powers added algebraically ($$P = P_1 + P_2$$).", "Violet light has the shortest wavelength and highest refractive index, hence deviates the most through a prism."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which type of lens is used to correct myopia (short-sightedness)?", "options": ["Concave lens", "Convex lens", "Cylindrical lens", "Bifocal lens"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Concave (diverging) lenses are used to diverge rays so they focus correctly on the retina."}]}'
