        3,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021'::uuid,
        5,
        'Unsaturation Tests and Isomerism Traps: Saturated hydrocarbons (alkanes) undergo substitution reactions and DO NOT decolorize reddish-brown bromine water or purple alkaline $$\ce{KMnO4}$$ (Baeyer''s reagent). Unsaturated hydrocarbons (alkenes with double bonds and alkynes with triple bonds) rapidly undergo addition reactions, decolorizing bromine water without catalyst. Another critical trap: Methane ($$\ce{CH4}$$), Ethane ($$\ce{C2H6}$$), and Propane ($$\ce{C3H8}$$) have ZERO structural isomers! The minimum carbon count required for structural chain isomerism in alkanes is FOUR ($$\ce{C4H10}$$, which forms n-butane and isobutane).',
        'Assuming propane ($$\ce{C3H8}$$) has chain isomers. It has only 1 structural form; isomerism begins strictly at butane ($$\ce{C4H10}$$).',
        '["Unsaturation Test: Alkenes/alkynes decolorize $$\\ce{Br2}$$ water (addition reaction)", "Saturated Hydrocarbons: Inactive towards $$\\ce{Br2}$$ water under normal conditions", "Chain Isomerism Threshold: Alkanes require at least 4 carbons ($$\\ce{C4H10}$$ has 2 isomers)", "Pentane ($$\\ce{C5H12}$$) Isomers: 3 (n-pentane, isopentane, neopentane)", "Hexane ($$\\ce{C6H14}$$) Isomers: 5 isomers"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A student is given two colorless liquid hydrocarbons in test tubes A and B: one is cyclohexane ($$\\ce{C6H12}$$) and the other is cyclohexene ($$\\ce{C6H10}$$). Describe how bromine water can chemically distinguish between them.", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify bonding nature: Cyclohexane ($$\\ce{C6H12}$$) is a cyclic saturated alkane containing only single C-C bonds. Cyclohexene ($$\\ce{C6H10}$$) is an unsaturated cyclic alkene possessing a reactive double bond ($$\\ce{C=C}$$).", "Step 2: Add bromine water ($$\\ce{Br2/H2O}$$, reddish-brown color): Cyclohexene undergoes an electrophilic addition reaction across the double bond, converting bromine into colorless 1,2-dibromocyclohexane. The reddish-brown color vanishes immediately.", "Step 3: Analyze cyclohexane reaction: Saturated cyclohexane cannot undergo addition; the reddish-brown color persists.", "Step 4: Conclusion: The test tube that decolorizes bromine water is cyclohexene."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Cyclohexene decolorizes bromine water; cyclohexane does not}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Classify Saturation", "explanation": "Cyclohexane is saturated; cyclohexene has C=C unsaturation.", "formula": "$$\\ce{C6H10} \\text{ (unsaturated)} \\text{ vs } \\ce{C6H12} \\text{ (saturated)}$$", "tip": "Look for double or triple bonds."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Evaluate Addition Reaction", "explanation": "Unsaturated alkenes add Br2 across double bond.", "formula": "$$\\ce{C6H10 + Br2 -> C6H10Br2} \\quad (\\text{colorless})$$", "tip": "Brown color discharges instantly."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Differentiate Saturated Compound", "explanation": "Alkanes do not react with bromine water without UV light.", "formula": "$$\\text{No reaction at room temp}$$", "tip": "Color remains reddish-brown."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Decolorization of bromine water is the definitive laboratory test for unsaturation.", "Alkanes require at least 4 carbon atoms to exhibit structural isomerism."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the minimum number of carbon atoms an alkane must possess to exhibit chain isomerism?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "2"], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "Butane ($$\\ce{C4H10}$$) is the smallest alkane with isomers (n-butane and 2-methylpropane/isobutane)."}]}'
    ),
