        2,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022'::uuid,
        6,
        'Neutralization Titration Stoichiometry & pH Calculation: In neutralization reactions, an acid reacts with a base to form salt and water. At the equivalence point, the gram-equivalents of acid equal the gram-equivalents of base: $$N_1 V_1 = N_2 V_2$$ or $$n_1 M_1 V_1 = n_2 M_2 V_2$$, where $$n$$ is the n-factor (basicity of acid or acidity of base). The pH scale measures hydrogen ion concentration: $$\text{pH} = -\log_{10}[\ce{H^+}]$$ and $$\text{pH} + \text{pOH} = 14$$ at $$25^\circ\text{C}$$.',
        'Assuming molarity equals normality for diprotic acids like sulfuric acid ($$\ce{H2SO4}$$). For $$\ce{H2SO4}$$, normality is twice molarity ($$N = 2M$$) because each molecule donates two protons.',
        '["Equivalence Law: $$N_{\\text{acid}} V_{\\text{acid}} = N_{\\text{base}} V_{\\text{base}}$$", "Normality & Molarity: $$N = M \\times n\\text{-factor}$$", "pH Definition: $$\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}[\\ce{H^+}]$$", "Water Dissociation ($$25^\\circ\\text{C}$$): $$[\\ce{H^+}][\\ce{OH^-}] = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14} \\implies \\text{pH} + \\text{pOH} = 14$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "What volume of $$0.1\\text{ M } \\ce{H2SO4}$$ solution is required to completely neutralize $$50\\text{ mL}$$ of $$0.2\\text{ M } \\ce{NaOH}$$ solution?", "steps": ["Step 1: Determine n-factor and normality of $$\\ce{H2SO4}$$: Sulfuric acid is diprotic ($$n=2$$). Normality $$N_1 = 0.1\\text{ M} \\times 2 = 0.2\\text{ N}$$.", "Step 2: Determine n-factor and normality of $$\\ce{NaOH}$$: Sodium hydroxide is monoacidic ($$n=1$$). Normality $$N_2 = 0.2\\text{ M} \\times 1 = 0.2\\text{ N}$$.", "Step 3: Apply the Law of Equivalents: $$N_1 V_1 = N_2 V_2$$.", "Step 4: Solve for $$V_1$$: $$0.2 \\times V_1 = 0.2 \\times 50 \\implies V_1 = 50\\text{ mL}$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$V = 50\\text{ mL}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Calculate Normality of Acid", "explanation": "H2SO4 donates 2 protons: N = M * 2.", "formula": "$$N_1 = 0.1 \\times 2 = 0.2\\text{ N}$$", "tip": "Always account for basicity."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Calculate Normality of Base", "explanation": "NaOH donates 1 hydroxide ion: N = M * 1.", "formula": "$$N_2 = 0.2 \\times 1 = 0.2\\text{ N}$$", "tip": "Monoacidic base."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Equate Equivalents", "explanation": "N1 * V1 = N2 * V2.", "formula": "$$0.2 \\times V_1 = 0.2 \\times 50 \\implies V_1 = 50\\text{ mL}$$", "tip": "Direct cancellation."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Always use Normality ($$N = M \\times n$$) when equating neutralization volumes.", "Diprotic acids ($$\\ce{H2SO4}$$) supply twice the proton equivalents of monoprotic acids ($$\\ce{HCl}$$) at the same molarity."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the pH of a 0.001 M HCl solution?", "options": ["3", "1", "11", "2"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "$$[\\ce{H^+}] = 10^{-3}\\text{ M} \\implies \\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(10^{-3}) = 3$$."}]}'
