        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000074'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Extremely Dilute Acid pH Trap & Salt Hydrolysis Nature: A notorious question asks for the pH of a $$10^{-8}\text{ M } \ce{HCl}$$ solution. Candidates blindly apply $$-\log(10^{-8}) = 8$$, declaring an acid to be basic! An acid solution can NEVER have a pH greater than 7 at $$25^\circ\text{C}$$. In ultra-dilute acids ($$< 10^{-6}\text{ M}$$), the auto-ionization of water ($$[\ce{H^+}]_{\text{water}} = 10^{-7}\text{ M}$$) CANNOT be neglected. Total $$[\ce{H^+}] = 10^{-8} + 10^{-7} = 1.1 \times 10^{-7}\text{ M}$$, yielding a true pH of approximately $$6.96$$ (slightly acidic). Another trap: Salt of strong acid + weak base ($$\ce{NH4Cl}$$) is acidic ($$\text{pH} < 7$$), while salt of weak acid + strong base ($$\ce{CH3COONa}$$) is basic ($$\text{pH} > 7$$).',
        'Calculating $$\text{pH} = 8$$ for $$10^{-8}\text{ M HCl}$$. An acid dissolved in water cannot make the solution alkaline.',
        '["Dilute Acid Rule: Total $$[\\ce{H^+}] = [\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{acid}} + [\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{water}}$$", "$$10^{-8}\\text{ M HCl}$$: True pH $$\\approx 6.96$$ (Always $$< 7$$)", "Strong Acid + Weak Base ($$\\ce{NH4Cl}$$): Acidic solution ($$\\text{pH} < 7$$)", "Weak Acid + Strong Base ($$\\ce{CH3COONa}$$): Basic solution ($$\\text{pH} > 7$$)", "Strong Acid + Strong Base ($$\\ce{NaCl}$$): Neutral solution ($$\\text{pH} = 7$$)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Calculate the true pH of a $$10^{-8}\\text{ M } \\ce{HCl}$$ aqueous solution at $$25^\\circ\\text{C}$$.", "steps": ["Step 1: Recognize water auto-ionization: Pure water contributes $$[\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{water}} = x$$ and $$[\\ce{OH^-}] = x$$. In the presence of $$10^{-8}\\text{ M } \\ce{HCl}$$, total $$[\\ce{H^+}] = (10^{-8} + x)$$.", "Step 2: Apply ionic product of water ($$K_w$$): $$K_w = [\\ce{H^+}][\\ce{OH^-}] = (x + 10^{-8})(x) = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14}$$.", "Step 3: Solve quadratic equation: $$x^2 + 10^{-8}x - 10^{-14} = 0$$. Solving for $$x$$ yields $$x \\approx 0.95 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$$.", "Step 4: Compute total $$[\\ce{H^+}]$$: Total $$[\\ce{H^+}] = 10^{-8} + 0.95 \\times 10^{-7} = 1.05 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$$.", "Step 5: Calculate pH: $$\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(1.05 \\times 10^{-7}) = 7 - \\log_{10}(1.05) \\approx 7 - 0.02 = 6.98$$. The solution is slightly acidic, as physically required."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{pH} \\approx 6.98 \\quad (\\text{Acidic, never } 8.0)$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Acknowledge Water Dissociation", "explanation": "Water produces 10^-7 M H+ which dominates over 10^-8 M.", "formula": "$$[\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{total}} = [\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{acid}} + [\\ce{H^+}]_{\\text{water}}$$", "tip": "Acid pH must remain below 7.0."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Solve Water Equilibrium", "explanation": "(x + 10^-8) * x = 10^-14.", "formula": "$$x \\approx 0.95 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$$", "tip": "Le Chatelier suppression of water ionization."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Calculate Logarithm", "explanation": "pH = -log(1.05 * 10^-7) ~ 6.98.", "formula": "$$\\text{pH} \\approx 6.98$$", "tip": "Just below neutral 7.0."}], "keyTakeaways": ["An acidic solution can never have a pH equal to or greater than 7 at room temperature.", "Salts of strong acids and weak bases undergo cationic hydrolysis, generating acidic solutions."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the nature of an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride (NH4Cl)?", "options": ["Acidic (pH < 7)", "Basic (pH > 7)", "Neutral (pH = 7)", "Amphoteric"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "$$\\ce{NH4Cl}$$ is the salt of a strong acid ($$\\ce{HCl}$$) and a weak base ($$\\ce{NH4OH}$$); it hydrolyzes to produce excess $$\\ce{H^+}$$, making the solution acidic."}]}'
