        'Common Chemical Salts & Indicator Color Transitions Reference Sheet: Comprehensive chemical formulas, manufacturing processes (Chlor-Alkali), and indicator color ranges for Litmus, Phenolphthalein, and Methyl Orange.',
        'Confusing Baking Soda ($$\ce{NaHCO3}$$) with Washing Soda ($$\ce{Na2CO3.10H2O}$$) or Baking Powder (Baking soda + edible tartaric acid).',
        '["Bleaching Powder: $$\\ce{CaOCl2}$$ (Calcium hypochlorite, oxidizing agent)", "Baking Soda: $$\\ce{NaHCO3}$$ (Sodium hydrogen carbonate)", "Washing Soda: $$\\ce{Na2CO3 . 10H2O}$$ (Sodium carbonate decahydrate)", "Plaster of Paris: $$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O}$$ (Calcium sulfate hemihydrate)", "Gypsum: $$\\ce{CaSO4 . 2H2O}$$ (Heated at 373K to make POP)", "Phenolphthalein Indicator: Colorless in Acid, Pink in Base", "Methyl Orange Indicator: Red/Pink in Acid, Yellow in Base", "Litmus Paper: Blue turns Red in Acid; Red turns Blue in Base"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Identify the chemical formula of Plaster of Paris and write the balanced chemical equation when it sets upon mixing with water.", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify chemical name and formula: Plaster of Paris is calcium sulfate hemihydrate, represented by $$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O}$$.", "Step 2: Chemical setting reaction: On mixing with water, it rehydrates to form hard gypsum ($$\\ce{CaSO4 . 2H2O}$$).", "Step 3: Write equation: $$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O + 1 1/2 H2O -> CaSO4 . 2H2O}$$. This exothermic crystallization is what causes the plaster to harden."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O + 1.5 H2O -> CaSO4 . 2H2O} \\quad (\\text{Hard Gypsum})$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "State Formula of POP", "explanation": "POP is hemihydrate.", "formula": "$$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O}$$", "tip": "Half water molecule per CaSO4 unit."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Rehydration Equation", "explanation": "Absorbs 1.5 molecules of water to reform gypsum.", "formula": "$$\\ce{CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O + 3/2 H2O -> CaSO4 . 2H2O}$$", "tip": "Sets in 10-15 minutes."}], "keyTakeaways": ["POP is formed by heating gypsum to $$373\\text{ K}$$ ($$100^\\circ\\text{C}$$); overheating produces ''dead burnt plaster'' ($$\\ce{CaSO4}$$).", "Baking powder contains tartaric acid to neutralize the bitter taste of sodium carbonate produced during baking."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the color of phenolphthalein in an aqueous solution of sodium hydroxide?", "options": ["Pink", "Colorless", "Yellow", "Red"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Phenolphthalein turns deep pink in basic solutions ($$\\text{pH} > 8.2$$)."}]}'
