        6,
        'Cell Cycle Phases & Mitosis vs Meiosis Chromosomal Numerics: The eukaryotic cell cycle consists of Interphase ($$G_1 \to S \to G_2$$) and M-phase (nuclear division followed by cytokinesis). In the $$S$$ (Synthesis) phase, DNA replicates, doubling DNA content from $$2C$$ to $$4C$$, while chromosome number remains invariant ($$2n$$). Mitosis produces two genetically identical diploid daughter cells ($$2n$$). Meiosis involves two successive divisions (Meiosis I - reductional, Meiosis II - equational), reducing chromosome count by half to yield four haploid gametes ($$n$$).',
        'Assuming chromosome number doubles during S-phase. Only the DNA quantity doubles ($$2C \to 4C$$); the chromosome count remains strictly $$2n$$ because sister chromatids remain attached at the centromere.',
        '["G1 Phase: Cell growth, RNA and protein synthesis ($$2n, 2C$$)", "S Phase: DNA replication ($$2n, 4C$$) — Chromosome number does NOT double", "G2 Phase: Pre-mitotic checkpoint and spindle protein synthesis ($$2n, 4C$$)", "Mitosis Division: $$1 \\times (2n) \\to 2 \\times (2n)$$ (Equational division)", "Meiosis Division: $$1 \\times (2n) \\to 4 \\times (n)$$ (Reductional division)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A diploid human cell has $$46\\text{ chromosomes}$$ ($$2n = 46$$) and a DNA content of $$2C = 6.6\\text{ pg}$$ in the $$G_1$$ phase. What are the chromosome number and DNA content of this cell at (a) Metaphase of Mitosis, and (b) Anaphase II of Meiosis?", "steps": ["Step 1: Track cell through S-phase: During S-phase, DNA replicates from $$2C = 6.6\\text{ pg}$$ to $$4C = 13.2\\text{ pg}$$. The chromosome number remains $$2n = 46$$.", "Step 2: Mitosis Metaphase: The cell is in M-phase before sister chromatid separation. Therefore, Chromosomes $$= 46$$, and DNA content $$= 13.2\\text{ pg}$$.", "Step 3: Meiosis progression: After Meiosis I, the cell becomes haploid ($$n = 23$$) with DNA content $$2C = 6.6\\text{ pg}$$.", "Step 4: Meiosis Anaphase II: At Anaphase II, sister chromatids split and are now individual chromosomes, so chromosome number temporarily doubles back to $$23 \\times 2 = 46\\text{ chromosomes}$$, but total DNA content in the cell is $$2C = 6.6\\text{ pg}$$.", "Step 5: Synthesize results: Metaphase of Mitosis: 46 chromosomes, 13.2 pg; Anaphase II: 46 chromosomes, 6.6 pg."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Mitosis Metaphase: } 46\\text{ chr, } 13.2\\text{ pg}; \\quad \\text{Anaphase II: } 46\\text{ chr, } 6.6\\text{ pg}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Track S-phase Doubling", "explanation": "DNA content doubles (6.6 pg to 13.2 pg); chromosome count unchanged at 46.", "formula": "$$2C \\to 4C = 13.2\\text{ pg}, \\quad 2n = 46$$", "tip": "Centromere count equals chromosome count."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Mitosis Metaphase Values", "explanation": "Before sister chromatids separate.", "formula": "$$46\\text{ chromosomes}, \\quad 13.2\\text{ pg}$$", "tip": "Equational division."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Meiosis Anaphase II Values", "explanation": "Centromeres split in haploid cells.", "formula": "$$46\\text{ temporary chromosomes}, \\quad 6.6\\text{ pg}$$", "tip": "Each daughter cell receives 23 chr, 3.3 pg."}], "keyTakeaways": ["DNA replication occurs strictly in the S-phase of Interphase, not during active mitosis.", "Meiosis I is the reductional division where homologous chromosomes separate; Meiosis II is equational."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "In which phase of the cell cycle does crossing-over occur?", "options": ["Pachytene of Prophase I", "Leptotene of Prophase I", "Metaphase II", "Anaphase I"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Crossing-over between non-sister chromatids occurs during the Pachytene stage of Prophase I in Meiosis."}]}'
