        5,
        'Prokaryotic vs Eukaryotic Organelles & Membrane Architecture Traps: Fundamental structural distinctions tested in examinations:
1. Prokaryotes (Bacteria, Cyanobacteria) lack ALL membrane-bound organelles (no nucleus, no mitochondria, no endoplasmic reticulum, no Golgi). Their ribosomes are strictly $$70\text{S}$$ ($$50\text{S} + 30\text{S}$$ subunits). Eukaryotes possess $$80\text{S}$$ ribosomes in cytoplasm ($$60\text{S} + 40\text{S}$$) but maintain $$70\text{S}$$ ribosomes inside Mitochondria and Chloroplasts (Endosymbiotic theory!).
2. Cell wall composition: Plant cell walls are made of Cellulose, Fungal cell walls of Chitin, and Bacterial cell walls of Peptidoglycan (murein). Animal cells have NO cell wall.',
        'Believing that eukaryotic cells contain ONLY 80S ribosomes. Eukaryotic mitochondria and chloroplasts contain prokaryote-like 70S ribosomes.',
        '["Prokaryotic Ribosomes: $$70\\text{S}$$ ($$50\\text{S} + 30\\text{S}$$)", "Eukaryotic Ribosomes: $$80\\text{S}$$ in cytosol, $$70\\text{S}$$ in mitochondria/chloroplasts", "Plant Cell Wall: Cellulose, hemicellulose, pectin", "Fungal Cell Wall: Chitin (polymer of N-acetylglucosamine)", "Bacterial Cell Wall: Peptidoglycan (Murein)", "Semi-Autonomous Organelles: Mitochondria and Chloroplasts (possess circular DNA & 70S ribosomes)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Which of the following cellular features is shared by BOTH a bacterium (prokaryote) and a human liver cell (eukaryote)?\n(A) Mitochondria\n(B) Nuclear envelope\n(C) Ribosomes\n(D) Endoplasmic Reticulum", "steps": ["Step 1: Check prokaryotic architecture: Bacteria are prokaryotic organisms. They lack membrane-bound organelles, eliminating Mitochondria, Nuclear envelope, and Endoplasmic reticulum.", "Step 2: Check universal components: Ribosomes are non-membrane-bound ribonucleoprotein particles responsible for protein translation. They are present in ALL living cells, both prokaryotes ($$70\\text{S}$$) and eukaryotes ($$80\\text{S}$$).", "Step 3: Conclude: Ribosomes are the universal feature shared by both."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{(C) Ribosomes}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Filter Membrane-Bound Organelles", "explanation": "Mitochondria, nucleus, and ER are restricted to eukaryotes.", "formula": "$$\\text{Organelles with membranes} = \\text{Absent in bacteria}$$", "tip": "Prokaryotes lack internal membrane compartmentalization."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Identify Universal Organelle", "explanation": "Ribosomes lack a lipid bilayer membrane.", "formula": "$$\\text{Ribosomes} \\implies \\text{Universal to all cellular life}$$", "tip": "Present from bacteria to humans."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Ribosomes are the only organelles found in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.", "Mitochondria and Chloroplasts have their own circular double-stranded DNA and 70S ribosomes, supporting the endosymbiotic theory."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the cell wall of fungi primarily composed of?", "options": ["Chitin", "Cellulose", "Peptidoglycan", "Lignin"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Fungal cell walls are composed of chitin, a fibrous substance consisting of polysaccharides."}]}'
