        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000025'::uuid,
        5,
        'The Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) vs Reverse Repo Trap & Liquidity Corridor: In April 2022, the RBI introduced the Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) as the primary tool to absorb excess systemic liquidity, replacing the fixed-rate Reverse Repo Rate as the floor of the LAF corridor. Critical difference: Under Reverse Repo, the RBI MUST provide Government Securities (G-Secs) as collateral to commercial banks. Under SDF, the RBI absorbs liquidity WITHOUT providing any collateral securities! The modern monetary corridor is: SDF (Floor, $$\text{Repo} - 25\text{ bps}$$) $$\leftrightarrow$$ Repo Rate (Target) $$\leftrightarrow$$ MSF (Ceiling, $$\text{Repo} + 25\text{ bps}$$).',
        'Believing Reverse Repo is still the operational floor of the LAF corridor. SDF replaced Reverse Repo as the floor, operating collateral-free.',
        '["SDF Operational Floor: $$\\text{Repo Rate} - 0.25\\% \\quad (\\text{No G-Sec collateral required})$$", "LAF Policy Anchor: $$\\text{Repo Rate (Set by 6-member MPC)}$$", "MSF Operational Ceiling: $$\\text{Repo Rate} + 0.25\\% \\quad (\\text{Emergency overnight borrowing})$$", "Corridor Width: Fixed symmetric spread of $$50\\text{ basis points}$$ ($$\\pm 25\\text{ bps}$$)", "Reverse Repo Status: Fixed Reverse Repo retained at $$3.35\\%$$ but rendered non-operational"]',
        '{"questionStem": "If the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) sets the Repo Rate at $$6.50\\%$$, what are the prevailing Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) rate and Marginal Standing Facility (MSF) rate under RBI''s liquidity framework?", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify the policy corridor symmetry: The RBI operating corridor is pegged at $$\\pm 25\\text{ basis points}$$ ($$\\pm 0.25\\%\\$$) around the policy Repo Rate.", "Step 2: Compute SDF Rate (Corridor Floor): $$\\text{SDF} = \\text{Repo} - 0.25\\% = 6.50\\% - 0.25\\% = 6.25\\%$$.", "Step 3: Compute MSF Rate (Corridor Ceiling): $$\\text{MSF} = \\text{Repo} + 0.25\\% = 6.50\\% + 0.25\\% = 6.75\\%$$.", "Step 4: Verify spread: The corridor spread between MSF and SDF is $$6.75\\% - 6.25\\% = 0.50\\%$$ ($$50\\text{ bps}$$)."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{SDF} = 6.25\\%, \\quad \\text{MSF} = 6.75\\%$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Recall Corridor Offsets", "explanation": "SDF is Repo - 25 bps; MSF is Repo + 25 bps.", "formula": "$$\\text{Floor} = \\text{Repo} - 0.25\\%, \\quad \\text{Ceiling} = \\text{Repo} + 0.25\\%$$", "tip": "Corridor width is 50 bps."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Calculate SDF Floor", "explanation": "6.50% - 0.25% = 6.25%.", "formula": "$$\\text{SDF} = 6.25\\%$$", "tip": "Collateral-free absorption."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Calculate MSF Ceiling", "explanation": "6.50% + 0.25% = 6.75%.", "formula": "$$\\text{MSF} = 6.75\\%$$", "tip": "Penal overnight window."}], "keyTakeaways": ["SDF absorbs surplus cash without tying up sovereign securities in the RBI''s balance sheet.", "MSF allows scheduled commercial banks to borrow overnight beyond their SLR quota up to a prescribed limit."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Does the RBI provide collateral securities to commercial banks when absorbing liquidity under SDF?", "options": ["No, SDF is completely uncollateralised", "Yes, in G-Secs only", "Yes, in Treasury bills only", "Yes, in gold"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "No! The defining feature of the Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) under Section 17 of the RBI Act is that it absorbs liquidity without requiring collateral."}]}'
