        'Monetary Policy & Regulatory Ratios Cheat Sheet: Statutory definitions, governing legislative acts, and reserve requirements for scheduled commercial banks in India.',
        'Confusing the legislative acts: CRR is mandated under Section 42 of the RBI Act 1934; SLR is mandated under Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act 1949.',
        '["Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR): Section 42, RBI Act 1934 (Maintained strictly in CASH with RBI, earns 0% interest)", "Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR): Section 24, Banking Regulation Act 1949 (Maintained in Gold, Cash, or Approved G-Secs with the bank itself)", "Repo Rate: Rate at which RBI lends short-term liquidity to banks against collateral", "Bank Rate: Long-term rediscounting rate under Section 49 of RBI Act 1934 (Aligned with MSF)", "Monetary Policy Committee (MPC): Section 45ZB of RBI Act 1934 (6 members: 3 RBI, 3 Govt appointed; RBI Governor has casting vote)", "Inflation Target: $$4\\% \\pm 2\\%$$ Consumer Price Index (CPI) combined"]',
        '{"questionStem": "State the governing legal acts and acceptable asset classes for maintaining the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) and Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR).", "steps": ["Step 1: Define CRR: Governed by Section 42(1) of the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934. Commercial banks must maintain a specified percentage of their Net Demand and Time Liabilities (NDTL) exclusively as cash balances deposited with the RBI. No interest is paid by the RBI on CRR balances.", "Step 2: Define SLR: Governed by Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949. Banks must maintain a specified percentage of NDTL in unencumbered approved liquid assets (Cash in hand, Gold, or approved Government Securities - G-Secs/T-Bills).", "Step 3: Key distinction: CRR is held with the RBI in cash; SLR is held with the bank itself in liquid earning assets."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{CRR: Sec 42 RBI Act (Cash with RBI); SLR: Sec 24 BR Act (G-Secs/Gold/Cash with bank)}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Identify CRR Mandate", "explanation": "Section 42 of RBI Act 1934.", "formula": "$$\\text{CRR} \\implies \\text{RBI Act 1934, Section 42}$$", "tip": "Held with RBI, 0% interest."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Identify SLR Mandate", "explanation": "Section 24 of Banking Regulation Act 1949.", "formula": "$$\\text{SLR} \\implies \\text{BR Act 1949, Section 24}$$", "tip": "Held with banks in liquid securities."}], "keyTakeaways": ["CRR is maintained under the RBI Act 1934; SLR is maintained under the Banking Regulation Act 1949.", "SLR earns interest yield (via government bonds), whereas CRR yields zero interest."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Under which act is the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) mandated?", "options": ["Banking Regulation Act 1949", "Reserve Bank of India Act 1934", "Negotiable Instruments Act 1881", "Companies Act 2013"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "SLR is mandated under Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949."}]}'
