        6,
        'CRAR and Risk-Weighted Assets (RWA) Calculation: The Capital to Risk-Weighted Assets Ratio (CRAR) measures a bank''s financial strength and loss-absorption capacity: $$\text{CRAR} = \frac{\text{Tier 1 Capital} + \text{Tier 2 Capital}}{\text{Risk-Weighted Assets (RWA)}} \times 100\%$$. RWA weights different balance-sheet assets by credit risk (e.g., Cash and G-Secs have $$0\%$$ risk weight; AAA corporate loans have $$20\%$$; unrated retail loans have $$100\%$$). In India, RBI mandates a minimum total CRAR of $$9.0\%$$ for Scheduled Commercial Banks ($$11.5\%$$ including the $$2.5\%$$ Capital Conservation Buffer - CCB).',
        'Using the global Basel III minimum total capital ratio ($$8.0\%$$) instead of the stricter RBI mandated norm for Indian scheduled commercial banks ($$9.0\%$$).',
        '["CRAR Formula: $$\\text{CRAR} = \\frac{\\text{Tier 1} + \\text{Tier 2}}{\\text{RWA}} \\times 100\\%$$", "RBI Minimum Total CRAR: $$9.0\\%$$ (excluding CCB)", "Capital Conservation Buffer (CCB): $$2.5\\%$$ in Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1)", "Total Minimum with CCB: $$11.5\\%$$", "RWA Calculation: $$\\text{RWA} = \\sum (\\text{Asset Value}_i \\times \\text{Risk Weight}_i)$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A commercial bank has Tier 1 Capital of Rs. $$8,000\\text{ crore}$$ and Tier 2 Capital of Rs. $$4,000\\text{ crore}$$. Its assets comprise: (1) Cash and G-Secs: Rs. $$20,000\\text{ crore}$$ (Risk weight $$0\\%$$); (2) AAA Corporate Loans: Rs. $$25,000\\text{ crore}$$ (Risk weight $$20\\%$$); (3) Retail Housing Loans: Rs. $$50,000\\text{ crore}$$ (Risk weight $$50\\%$$); (4) Unrated Commercial Loans: Rs. $$45,000\\text{ crore}$$ (Risk weight $$100\\%$$). Calculate the bank''s RWA and CRAR.", "steps": ["Step 1: Compute Risk-Weighted Assets (RWA):\n- Cash & G-Secs: $$20000 \\times 0\\% = 0$$\n- AAA Loans: $$25000 \\times 20\\% = 5000$$\n- Housing Loans: $$50000 \\times 50\\% = 25000$$\n- Commercial Loans: $$45000 \\times 100\\% = 45000$$\n- Total RWA $$= 0 + 5000 + 25000 + 45000 = \\text{Rs. } 75,000\\text{ crore}$$.", "Step 2: Compute Total Eligible Capital: Total Capital $$= \\text{Tier 1} + \\text{Tier 2} = 8000 + 4000 = \\text{Rs. } 12,000\\text{ crore}$$.", "Step 3: Calculate CRAR: $$\\text{CRAR} = \\frac{12000}{75000} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{12}{75} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{4}{25} \\times 100\\% = 16.0\\%$$.", "Step 4: Compliance check: The bank''s CRAR of $$16.0\\%$$ comfortably exceeds the RBI regulatory requirement of $$11.5\\%$$ (including CCB)."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{RWA} = \\text{Rs. } 75,000\\text{ cr}, \\quad \\text{CRAR} = 16.0\\% \\quad (\\text{Compliant})$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Weight Assets by Credit Risk", "explanation": "Multiply each asset class by its risk weight percentage.", "formula": "$$\\text{RWA} = 0 + 5000 + 25000 + 45000 = 75,000$$", "tip": "Cash and sovereign securities have zero risk weight."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Sum Eligible Capital", "explanation": "Tier 1 + Tier 2 capital.", "formula": "$$8000 + 4000 = 12,000\\text{ cr}$$", "tip": "Tier 2 cannot exceed Tier 1."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Evaluate Capital Adequacy Ratio", "explanation": "12000 / 75000 * 100%.", "formula": "$$\\text{CRAR} = 16.0\\%$$", "tip": "Exceeds RBI 11.5% hurdle."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Risk-Weighted Assets normalize balance sheets so high-risk loans require proportionally more equity backing.", "Sovereign government securities have a zero risk weight in domestic currency."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What is the minimum CRAR mandated by the RBI for Indian public and private scheduled commercial banks (excluding CCB)?", "options": ["9.0%", "8.0%", "10.5%", "11.5%"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "RBI mandates a minimum CRAR of 9.0% for commercial banks (1% higher than the Basel III global standard of 8.0%)."}]}'
