        5,
        'Tier 1 vs Tier 2 Capital Hierarchy & AT1 Bond Write-Down Traps: Capital under Basel III is strictly tiered based on loss-absorbency during going-concern vs gone-concern resolution:
1. Tier 1 Capital (Going-Concern): Absorbs losses while the bank continues operating. Consists of Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1: equity shares, retained earnings) and Additional Tier 1 (AT1: perpetual non-cumulative preference shares, perpetual bonds). Crucial exam trap: AT1 bonds feature a ''Point of Non-Viability (PONV)'' clause where they can be fully written down or converted to equity before common shareholders lose everything (as demonstrated in the Yes Bank resolution!).
2. Tier 2 Capital (Gone-Concern): Absorbs losses in insolvency/liquidation (subordinated debt, general provisions). Tier 2 capital is strictly capped at $$100\%$$ of Tier 1 capital.',
        'Assuming Additional Tier 1 (AT1) bonds are conventional debt instruments with guaranteed principal return. AT1 bonds can be completely written off if the bank breaches PONV triggers.',
        '["Tier 1 Capital: Going-Concern Capital (CET1 + AT1)", "Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1): Minimum $$5.5\\%$$ (Core equity + statutory reserves)", "AT1 Bonds: Non-cumulative perpetual debt with loss write-down features", "Tier 2 Capital: Gone-Concern Capital (Subordinated debt, revaluation reserves with 55% discount)", "Tier 2 Ceiling: Eligible Tier 2 cannot exceed $$100\\%$$ of Tier 1 Capital"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A bank has Tier 1 Capital of Rs. $$3,000\\text{ crore}$$ and Tier 2 subordinated debt of Rs. $$4,000\\text{ crore}$$. If its RWA is Rs. $$50,000\\text{ crore}$$, what is the bank''s recognized Total Eligible Capital and CRAR for regulatory compliance?", "steps": ["Step 1: Check the regulatory cap on Tier 2 Capital: Under Basel III guidelines, eligible Tier 2 capital cannot exceed $$100\\%$$ of eligible Tier 1 capital.", "Step 2: Determine recognized Tier 2: Since Tier 1 is Rs. $$3,000\\text{ crore}$$, the maximum recognized Tier 2 capital is capped at Rs. $$3,000\\text{ crore}$$. The excess Rs. $$1,000\\text{ crore}$$ of Tier 2 is disallowed for CRAR calculation.", "Step 3: Calculate Total Recognized Capital: Total Capital $$= 3000\\text{ (Tier 1)} + 3000\\text{ (capped Tier 2)} = \\text{Rs. } 6,000\\text{ crore}$$.", "Step 4: Compute recognized CRAR: $$\\text{CRAR} = \\frac{6000}{50000} \\times 100\\% = 12.0\\%$$. Note: Naively adding all 4,000 cr Tier 2 would give an invalid $$14.0\\%$$."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Total Eligible Capital} = \\text{Rs. } 6,000\\text{ cr}, \\quad \\text{CRAR} = 12.0\\%$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Apply Tier 2 Regulatory Ceiling", "explanation": "Eligible Tier 2 is capped at 100% of Tier 1.", "formula": "$$\\text{Tier 2 Ceiling} = \\text{Tier 1} = 3,000\\text{ cr}$$", "tip": "Excess subordinated debt cannot be counted."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Calculate Allowed Capital", "explanation": "3000 + 3000 = 6000 crore.", "formula": "$$\\text{Total Capital} = 6,000\\text{ cr}$$", "tip": "Tier 1 is the primary anchor."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Calculate CRAR", "explanation": "6000 / 50000 * 100%.", "formula": "$$\\text{CRAR} = 12.0\\%$$", "tip": "Complies with regulatory caps."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Tier 2 capital is capped at 100% of Tier 1 capital for regulatory CRAR computation.", "Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1) is the highest quality capital with maximum loss absorbency."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which of the following capital components represents ''Going-Concern'' capital under Basel III?", "options": ["Tier 1 Capital", "Tier 2 Capital", "Subordinated Debt", "General Provisions"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Tier 1 capital represents ''Going-Concern'' capital, designed to absorb losses while the bank continues normal business operations."}]}'
