        6,
        'Constitutional Jurisprudence of the Preamble & Amending Power: The legal status of the Preamble evolved through three landmark Supreme Court rulings:
1. Berubari Union Case (1960): SC held that the Preamble is NOT a part of the Constitution.
2. Kesavananda Bharati Case (1973): A historic 13-judge bench overruled Berubari, holding that the Preamble IS an integral part of the Constitution and can be amended under Article 368, subject to the ''Basic Structure Doctrine''.
3. LIC of India Case (1995): Reaffirmed that the Preamble is an integral part of the Constitution. To date, the Preamble has been amended exactly ONCE—by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976, which added three new words: ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', and ''Integrity''.',
        'Believing the Preamble has been amended multiple times, or that the word ''Democratic'' was added by the 42nd Amendment. Only ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', and ''Integrity'' were added.',
        '["Berubari Union (1960): Preamble is NOT part of Constitution", "Kesavananda Bharati (1973): Preamble IS an integral part; subject to Basic Structure", "LIC of India (1995): Reaffirmed as an integral part", "42nd Amendment Act 1976: Added ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', and ''Integrity''", "Amendment Frequency: Amended strictly ONCE in constitutional history"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Trace the constitutional amendment that modified the Preamble of the Indian Constitution, identify the three words inserted, and state the governing landmark judicial ruling.", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify the amendment act: The Preamble was amended by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 (during the Emergency under the Indira Gandhi administration).", "Step 2: Identify the three words inserted: The original text ''Sovereign Democratic Republic'' was changed to ''Sovereign Socialist Secular Democratic Republic'', and ''Unity of the Nation'' was changed to ''Unity and Integrity of the Nation''. The three newly inserted words are: ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', and ''Integrity''.", "Step 3: Identify the constitutional basis: Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973) established that the Preamble is part of the Constitution and amendable under Article 368 without altering the Basic Structure."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{42nd Amendment (1976): Added ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', ''Integrity''; Kesavananda Bharati (1973)}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Name the Amendment", "explanation": "42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976.", "formula": "$$\\text{42nd CAA, 1976}$$", "tip": "Often called the ''Mini-Constitution''."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "List the Three Added Words", "explanation": "Socialist, Secular, and Integrity.", "formula": "$$\\text{''Socialist'', ''Secular'', ''Integrity''}$$", "tip": "Remember: 2 in description, 1 in fraternity."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Cite Judicial Authority", "explanation": "Kesavananda Bharati overruled Berubari Union.", "formula": "$$\\text{Kesavananda Bharati (1973)}$$", "tip": "13-judge constitutional bench."}], "keyTakeaways": ["The Preamble has been amended only once in history: by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976.", "Kesavananda Bharati (1973) ruled that the Preamble is an integral part of the Constitution."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which of the following words was NOT added to the Preamble by the 42nd Amendment Act of 1976?", "options": ["Democratic", "Socialist", "Secular", "Integrity"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "''Democratic'' was already present in the original 1950 Preamble. Only ''Socialist'', ''Secular'', and ''Integrity'' were added in 1976."}]}'
