        5,
        'Justiciability & Legislative Power Traps of the Preamble: Two profound legal characteristics of the Preamble are frequently trapped in UPSC and State PCS examinations:
1. Non-Justiciable Nature: The provisions of the Preamble are NOT enforceable in courts of law. A citizen cannot file a writ petition or lawsuit alleging a violation of the Preamble alone.
2. Legislative Power Status: The Preamble is NEITHER a source of power to the legislature NOR a prohibition upon the powers of the legislature. Parliamentary acts derive authority from substantive articles (e.g., Article 246, Lists I-III), not from the Preamble. Additionally, note the exact canonical sequence: ''Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic'' (SO-SO-SE-DE-RE).',
        'Assuming the Preamble is enforceable in court or serves as a source of legislative restriction upon Parliament.',
        '["Enforceability: NON-JUSTICIABLE (Cannot be enforced in court)", "Legislative Authority: Neither a source of power nor a source of limitation", "Canonical Order: Sovereign $$\\to$$ Socialist $$\\to$$ Secular $$\\to$$ Democratic $$\\to$$ Republic", "Adopted Date: 26 November 1949 (Constituent Assembly)", "Commencement Date: 26 January 1950 (Enacted with full Constitution)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Consider the following statements regarding the Preamble:\n1. It is directly enforceable in a court of law.\n2. It acts as a constitutional limitation on legislative powers of Parliament.\n3. The exact sequence of adjectives defining the nature of the Indian State is ''Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic''.\nWhich statements are correct?", "steps": ["Step 1: Evaluate Statement 1: The Preamble is non-justiciable; its provisions cannot be enforced in courts of law. Statement 1 is FALSE.", "Step 2: Evaluate Statement 2: The Supreme Court held that the Preamble is neither a source of power nor a prohibition/limitation upon legislative powers. Statement 2 is FALSE.", "Step 3: Evaluate Statement 3: The opening declaration reads ''SOVEREIGN SOCIALIST SECULAR DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC''. This matches the sequence exactly. Statement 3 is TRUE.", "Step 4: Conclude: Only statement 3 is correct."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Only Statement 3 is correct}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Test Justiciability", "explanation": "Preamble is non-justiciable like Directive Principles.", "formula": "$$\\text{Enforceability} = \\text{False}$$", "tip": "No writ lies for Preamble alone."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Test Legislative Power Status", "explanation": "Neither empowers nor prohibits legislative enactment.", "formula": "$$\\text{Source/Limitation} = \\text{False}$$", "tip": "Substantive articles govern authority."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Verify Canonical Sequence", "explanation": "Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic.", "formula": "$$\\text{SO-SO-SE-DE-RE} = \\text{True}$$", "tip": "Memorize the sequence acronym."}], "keyTakeaways": ["The Preamble is non-justiciable: no court can enforce its principles directly against state action.", "The Preamble provides interpretive guidance when constitutional provisions are ambiguous."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Is the Preamble to the Indian Constitution justiciable in a court of law?", "options": ["No, it is non-justiciable", "Yes, under Article 32", "Yes, under Article 226", "Only in the Supreme Court"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "The Preamble is non-justiciable; it cannot be enforced in any court of law."}]}'
