        'CORE',
        2,
        'b1010000-0000-0000-0000-000000000028'::uuid,
        6,
        'The Five Prerogative Writs and Jurisdictional Scope (Article 32 vs 226): Under Article 32 (Supreme Court) and Article 226 (High Courts), the judiciary issues five constitutional writs to enforce Fundamental Rights:
1. Habeas Corpus (''To have the body''): Against unlawful detention by state or private individuals.
2. Mandamus (''We command''): Directs a public official to perform a mandatory statutory duty. Cannot be issued against the President or Governor.
3. Prohibition: Issued by higher court to lower court/tribunal to prevent exceeding jurisdiction.
4. Certiorari (''To be certified''): Quashes an order already passed by a lower court/tribunal without jurisdiction or in violation of natural justice.
5. Quo-Warranto (''By what authority''): Inquires into the legality of a person''s claim to a public office.
Crucial distinction: High Court writ jurisdiction under Article 226 is WIDER than Supreme Court under Article 32, because High Courts can issue writs for Fundamental Rights AND ordinary legal rights!',
        'Believing the Supreme Court''s writ jurisdiction under Article 32 is wider than the High Courts'' under Article 226. Article 226 is wider because it extends to non-fundamental legal rights.',
        '["Habeas Corpus: Unlawful detention; lies against public AND private persons", "Mandamus: Compels public duty; does NOT lie against President, Governor, or private bodies", "Prohibition: Preventive writ issued to judicial/quasi-judicial bodies", "Certiorari: Curative writ to quash existing orders of judicial/quasi-judicial bodies", "Quo-Warranto: Prevents illegal usurpation of public statutory office", "Jurisdiction Width: Article 226 (High Court) > Article 32 (Supreme Court)"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A citizen is denied admission to an examination by a municipal school authority that refused to perform its mandatory statutory duty. Furthermore, a private company detains an employee unlawfully. Identify the appropriate writs applicable in each scenario.", "steps": ["Step 1: Analyze Scenario 1 (School authority): A municipal school authority is a public body under Article 12, failing to execute an obligatory statutory duty. The appropriate writ to compel a public official/body to execute a mandatory duty is Mandamus (''We command'').", "Step 2: Analyze Scenario 2 (Unlawful private detention): An individual is confined without lawful justification by a private company. Habeas Corpus (''To have the body'') is the ONLY writ that can be issued against BOTH public authorities and private individuals.", "Step 3: Synthesize result: Scenario 1 invokes Mandamus; Scenario 2 invokes Habeas Corpus."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Scenario 1: Mandamus; Scenario 2: Habeas Corpus}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Identify Public Duty Violation", "explanation": "Refusal by municipal authority to perform statutory duty invokes Mandamus.", "formula": "$$\\text{Mandamus} \\implies \\text{Public duty compulsion}$$", "tip": "Requires an existing legal right and corresponding public duty."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Identify Private Detention Remedy", "explanation": "Habeas Corpus lies against private entities.", "formula": "$$\\text{Habeas Corpus} \\implies \\text{Public and Private}$$", "tip": "The ultimate safeguard of individual liberty."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Habeas Corpus can be issued against private individuals as well as public authorities.", "Article 32 is itself a Fundamental Right (Right to Constitutional Remedies), which Dr. Ambedkar termed the ''Heart and Soul of the Constitution''."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which writ can be issued against private individuals as well as public authorities?", "options": ["Habeas Corpus", "Mandamus", "Certiorari", "Prohibition"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Habeas Corpus can be issued against both public authorities and private individuals who detain anyone unlawfully."}]}'
