        'Failing to recognize that the noun immediately after the comma MUST be the agent performing the introductory participial action.',
        '["Dangling Participle: Introductory verbal phrase lacks its logical subject following the comma", "Rule: $$\\text{[Participial Phrase]}, \\; \\mathbf{\\text{Logical Agent}} + \\text{Verb} \\dots$$", "Correction 1: Supply the logical agent as the subject of the main clause", "Correction 2: Expand participle into full subordinate clause with its own subject ($$\\text{While } S + V$$)", "Squinting Modifier: Modifier placed ambiguously between two elements it could modify"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Identify the grammatical error and provide two correct revisions: ''Having finished the examination, the papers were collected by the invigilator.''", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify introductory phrase: ''Having finished the examination'' is a perfect participial phrase describing the completion of the test.", "Step 2: Inspect subject after the comma: The noun immediately following the comma is ''the papers''. Papers cannot finish an examination! Therefore, ''Having finished'' is a classic dangling participle.", "Step 3: Revision 1 (Active Subject): Place the logical agent (the students / candidates) directly after the comma: ''Having finished the examination, the students submitted their papers to the invigilator.''", "Step 4: Revision 2 (Subordinate Clause): Expand into a dependent clause: ''After the students had finished the examination, the invigilator collected the papers.''"], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Dangling modifier; Correct: ''After the students had finished the exam, the invigilator collected the papers.''}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Isolate Participial Phrase", "explanation": "''Having finished the examination'' lacks an agent.", "formula": "$$\\text{Participial Phrase}$$", "tip": "Who finished the exam?"}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Check Grammatical Subject", "explanation": "''The papers'' follows comma, creating absurdity.", "formula": "$$\\text{Papers} \\ne \\text{Agent of finishing}$$", "tip": "Papers cannot finish exams."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Apply Structural Repair", "explanation": "Provide correct agent or create adverbial clause.", "formula": "$$\\text{Subordinate clause with explicit subject}$$", "tip": "Standard fix in SSC CGL error spotting."}], "keyTakeaways": ["The subject of an introductory participial phrase must be the noun placed immediately after the comma.", "Passive voice in the main clause frequently creates dangling modifier errors."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which of the following sentences is grammatically correct?", "options": ["Barking loudly, the mailman ran away from the dog.", "Barking loudly, the dog chased the mailman away.", "Barking loudly, the fence was jumped by the dog.", "Barking loudly, the mail was dropped by the mailman."], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "In (B), the dog is the logical agent performing the barking, so ''Barking loudly'' correctly modifies ''the dog''."}]}'
