        '{"questionStem": "Spot the error in the sentence: ''The committee was informed that the candidate only submitted his credentials yesterday.''", "steps": ["Step 1: Analyze intended meaning: The sentence intends to emphasize that the credentials were submitted as recently as yesterday, restricting the timeframe.", "Step 2: Inspect placement of ''only'': ''Only'' is placed directly before the verb ''submitted''. This grammatically implies the candidate did nothing other than submitting (he didn''t review, verify, or sign them).", "Step 3: Correct placement: Move ''only'' adjacent to the adverb of time ''yesterday'': ''The committee was informed that the candidate submitted his credentials only yesterday.''"], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Misplaced ''only''; Correct: ''...submitted his credentials only yesterday.''}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Identify Intended Focus", "explanation": "Restriction applies to timeframe ''yesterday''.", "formula": "$$\\text{Focus} = \\text{''yesterday''}$$", "tip": "Identify what is being limited."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Relocate Limiting Adverb", "explanation": "Place ''only'' directly before ''yesterday''.", "formula": "$$\\text{''only yesterday''}$$", "tip": "Adjacent placement preserves semantic intent."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Place limiting adverbs (''only'', ''almost'', ''just'') immediately adjacent to the specific word they modify.", "Misplacing limiting adverbs creates comic ambiguity or distorts factual meaning."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "Which sentence correctly conveys that the speaker possesses no more than three books?", "options": ["I only have three books.", "I have only three books.", "Only I have three books.", "I have three only books."], "correctOptionIndex": 1, "explanation": "''I have only three books'' places ''only'' directly before the quantity ''three books'', precisely restricting the count."}]}'
