        '["Rule 1 (Proximity): Keep modifiers as close as possible to the words they modify", "Rule 2 (Introductory Participles): The subject of the sentence MUST perform the participle action", "Rule 3 (Squinting Modifiers): Avoid placing adverbs between two verbs (e.g., ''Exercising regularly improves health'' vs ''Regularly exercising'')", "Rule 4 (Misplaced Prepositional Phrases): ''The girl saw the dog with a telescope'' $$\\to$$ ''With a telescope, the girl saw the dog''", "Rule 5 (Split Infinitives): Avoid inserting long adverbs between ''to'' and the verb in formal writing"]',
        '{"questionStem": "Identify the ambiguity in: ''Students who study often score higher grades.'' How can this squinting modifier be eliminated?", "steps": ["Step 1: Identify squinting adverb: The adverb ''often'' sits directly between ''study'' and ''score''.", "Step 2: Ambiguity analysis: Does ''often'' modify ''study'' (students who study frequently) or does it modify ''score'' (students frequently achieve higher grades)?", "Step 3: Resolve ambiguity:\n- Interpretation A: ''Students who often study score higher grades.''\n- Interpretation B: ''Students who study score higher grades often.''"], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Squinting modifier ''often''; resolve by placing next to ''study'' or ''score''}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Detect Squinting Position", "explanation": "''Often'' is caught between two verbs: study and score.", "formula": "$$\\text{Verb}_1 + \\mathbf{\\text{Adverb}} + \\text{Verb}_2$$", "tip": "Classic squinting modifier signature."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Anchor to Intended Verb", "explanation": "Move adverb to unambiguously modify one action.", "formula": "$$\\text{''Students who study frequently score higher grades.''}$$", "tip": "Eliminates dual interpretation."}], "keyTakeaways": ["A squinting modifier looks in both directions, making sentence interpretation ambiguous.", "Clarity in formal writing demands that adverbs clearly modify a single target."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What type of error is present in: ''Running quickly through the park, my keys fell out of my pocket''?", "options": ["Dangling modifier", "Squinting modifier", "Split infinitive", "Subject-verb disagreement"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Dangling modifier: ''my keys'' cannot run through the park. It should be: ''While I was running quickly through the park, my keys fell...''"}]}'
