        6,
        'Collision vs Broadcast Domains & Subnet Capacity Calculations: In network topology design:
- Hub (Layer 1): Single collision domain, single broadcast domain across all ports.
- Switch (Layer 2): Each port is an ISOLATED collision domain ($$N$$ ports $$= N$$ collision domains), but all ports remain in ONE single broadcast domain (unless partitioned by VLANs).
- Router (Layer 3): Breaks BOTH collision domains and broadcast domains per interface ($$M$$ interfaces $$= M$$ collision domains and $$M$$ broadcast domains).
For IPv4 subnetting: Given prefix length $$/n$$, host bits $$h = 32 - n$$. Usable host IPs per subnet is strictly $$2^h - 2$$ (subtracting network address and directed broadcast address).',
        'Forgetting to subtract 2 for the Network ID and Broadcast ID when calculating usable host addresses in an IPv4 subnet.',
        '["Hub: 1 Collision Domain, 1 Broadcast Domain", "Switch (N ports): N Collision Domains, 1 Broadcast Domain", "Router (M ports): M Collision Domains, M Broadcast Domains", "Usable Hosts Formula: $$N_{\\text{usable}} = 2^{32 - n} - 2$$", "Total Subnets from Subnet Bits $$s$$: $$N_{\\text{subnets}} = 2^s$$"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A network topology consists of a Router connected to two 24-port Switches. Switch 1 has 15 PCs connected; Switch 2 has 20 PCs connected. (a) How many collision domains and broadcast domains exist? (b) If each switch network is assigned a $$/27$$ IPv4 subnet, how many usable host IP addresses are available in each subnet?", "steps": ["Step 1: Calculate Broadcast Domains: Routers break broadcast domains. The router connects to two distinct switch interfaces, creating exactly 2 broadcast domains.", "Step 2: Calculate Collision Domains: Switches break collision domains per active port. Switch 1 has 15 PCs + 1 uplink to router $$= 16$$ collision domains. Switch 2 has 20 PCs + 1 uplink to router $$= 21$$ collision domains. Total collision domains $$= 16 + 21 = 37$$ collision domains.", "Step 3: Calculate usable hosts in a /27 subnet: In IPv4, total bits $$= 32$$. For a $$/27$$ subnet, host bits $$h = 32 - 27 = 5\\text{ bits}$$.", "Step 4: Compute usable host IPs: $$2^h - 2 = 2^5 - 2 = 32 - 2 = 30\\text{ usable host IPs}$$. (The first address is the Network ID, and the last address is the Broadcast ID)."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{37 Collision Domains, 2 Broadcast Domains; 30 Usable Hosts per /27 subnet}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Count Broadcast Domains", "explanation": "Router separates broadcast traffic per interface.", "formula": "$$\\text{Broadcast Domains} = 2$$", "tip": "Switches do not break broadcast domains without VLANs."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Count Collision Domains", "explanation": "Each active port on a switch is a separate collision domain.", "formula": "$$\\text{Collision Domains} = 16 + 21 = 37$$", "tip": "Count PC ports plus router uplink ports."}, {"stepNumber": 3, "title": "Compute Subnet Host Capacity", "explanation": "32 - 27 = 5 host bits; 2^5 - 2 = 30.", "formula": "$$2^5 - 2 = 30\\text{ usable hosts}$$", "tip": "Always subtract 2 for network and broadcast."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Switches break collision domains but forward broadcasts.", "Routers break both collision domains and broadcast domains.", "A /27 subnet provides 30 usable host addresses."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "How many usable host IP addresses can be configured on a /26 IPv4 subnet?", "options": ["62", "64", "30", "126"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "Host bits $$h = 32 - 26 = 6$$. Usable hosts $$= 2^6 - 2 = 64 - 2 = 62$$."}]}'
