        5,
        'Switching vs Routing Forwarding Mechanics & Loop Traps: Layer 2 Switches and Layer 3 Routers forward data using fundamentally different paradigms:
1. Switch (Layer 2): Operates on 48-bit MAC addresses stored in a Content Addressable Memory (CAM) table. If a destination MAC is not in the CAM table, the switch FLOODS the frame out of all ports except the ingress port (Unknown Unicast Flooding). Redundant switch links without Spanning Tree Protocol (STP - IEEE 802.1D) cause catastrophic Broadcast Storms.
2. Router (Layer 3): Operates on 32-bit/128-bit IP addresses using a routing table. Crucial trap: Routers DO NOT flood unknown packets—if no route matches, the packet is DROPPED and an ICMP ''Destination Unreachable'' is returned. Additionally, routers decrement the Time To Live (TTL) field by 1; if $$TTL = 0$$, the packet is discarded, preventing permanent loops.',
        'Assuming Layer 2 switches decrement TTL or drop unknown unicast frames. Switches do NOT modify TTL and flood unknown unicast frames to all ports.',
        '["Layer 2 Forwarding: Based on MAC address table (CAM); floods unknown unicast", "Layer 3 Forwarding: Based on IP routing table; drops unknown destinations", "Loop Prevention L2: Spanning Tree Protocol (STP, 802.1D) blocks redundant paths", "Loop Prevention L3: Time To Live (TTL) field decremented at each hop", "Broadcast Handling: Switches forward broadcasts; Routers block broadcasts"]',
        '{"questionStem": "A packet arrives at a networking device. The destination address is not found in its internal forwarding table. Describe the contrasting response of (a) a Layer 2 Switch, and (b) a Layer 3 Router.", "steps": ["Step 1: Analyze Layer 2 Switch behavior: A switch maintains a MAC address (CAM) table. When a frame arrives with an unknown destination MAC address, the switch performs ''Unknown Unicast Flooding'', copying and transmitting the frame out of every active port except the port on which it arrived.", "Step 2: Analyze Layer 3 Router behavior: A router maintains an IP routing table. When a packet arrives with a destination IP that has no matching entry (and no default route $$0.0.0.0/0$$), the router DROPS the packet immediately and transmits an ICMP Type 3 (Destination Unreachable) message back to the sender.", "Step 3: Conclude key operational divergence: Switches flood unknown destinations; Routers drop unknown destinations."], "finalAnswer": "$$\\text{Switch: Floods frame to all other ports; Router: Drops packet and sends ICMP unreachable}$$", "structuredSteps": [{"stepNumber": 1, "title": "Explain Switch Flooding", "explanation": "Unknown unicast flooded to discover host location.", "formula": "$$\\text{Switch} \\implies \\text{Floods frame to all ports}$$", "tip": "CAM table learning mechanism."}, {"stepNumber": 2, "title": "Explain Router Drop", "explanation": "Unmatched destination dropped to prevent wide-area flooding.", "formula": "$$\\text{Router} \\implies \\text{Drops packet + ICMP}$$", "tip": "Prevents Internet-wide congestion."}], "keyTakeaways": ["Layer 2 switches flood unknown unicast traffic; routers never flood packets.", "The TTL field in IP headers prevents packets from looping forever in networks."], "quickCheckItems": [{"question": "What happens to an IPv4 packet when its Time-To-Live (TTL) field decrements to zero?", "options": ["It is dropped and an ICMP Time Exceeded message is generated", "It is forwarded to the default gateway", "It is broadcast to all hosts on the subnet", "It is retransmitted by the sender"], "correctOptionIndex": 0, "explanation": "When TTL reaches 0, the router discards the packet and sends an ICMP Type 11 (Time Exceeded) message to the source."}]}'
